const DAILY_SOLUTIONS = {
 "S1-1": "<p>(a) Add real parts and imaginary parts separately:<br>$$(5 + 3i) + (-2 + 7i) = (5 - 2) + (3 + 7)i = 3 + 10i.$$<br>(b) The displacement from B to A is A&#x27;s position minus B&#x27;s position:<br>$$(5 + 3i) - (-2 + 7i) = (5 + 2) + (3 - 7)i = 7 - 4i.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (a) $3 + 10i$. (b) $7 - 4i$, meaning Drone A is 7 meters east and 4 meters south of Drone B.</strong></p>",
 "S1-2": "<p>By the Remainder Theorem, the remainder on division by $x - 3$ is $R(3)$:<br>$$R(3) = 2(3)^3 - 3(3)^2 + 4(3) + 5 = 54 - 27 + 12 + 5 = 44.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the remainder is 44. Since $R$ is measured in hundreds of dollars, this says the third performance itself earns a profit of $44 \\times 100 = \\$4{,}400$ — the remainder is exactly the polynomial&#x27;s value at $x = 3$.</strong></p>",
 "S1-3": "<p>Maya&#x27;s rate is $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mural per hour and Jordan&#x27;s is $\\frac{1}{4}$ per hour. Working together for $t$ hours, they complete one mural:<br>$$\\frac{t}{6} + \\frac{t}{4} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{2t + 3t}{12} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 5t = 12 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = \\frac{12}{5} = 2.4 \\text{ hours}.$$<br>Check: $2.4 &lt; 4$, so together they finish faster than the faster painter alone, as expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: together they paint the mural in 2.4 hours, i.e., 2 hours 24 minutes.</strong></p>",
 "S1-4": "<p>Factor the first denominator: $x^2 - x - 6 = (x - 3)(x + 2)$. Rewrite the second fraction with the common denominator $(x - 3)(x + 2)$:<br>$$\\frac{x^2 + 3x}{(x-3)(x+2)} - \\frac{x(x + 2)}{(x-3)(x+2)} = \\frac{x^2 + 3x - x^2 - 2x}{(x-3)(x+2)} = \\frac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the difference is $\\dfrac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}$, with excluded values $x = 3$ and $x = -2$ (the values that make either original denominator zero).</strong></p>",
 "S1-5": "<p>(a) Solve for $L$: divide by $2\\pi$, then square both sides, then multiply by $g$:<br>$$\\frac{T}{2\\pi} = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{T^2}{4\\pi^2} = \\frac{L}{g} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; L = \\frac{gT^2}{4\\pi^2}.$$<br>(b) Substitute $T = 2$ and $g = 9.8$:<br>$$L = \\frac{9.8(2)^2}{4\\pi^2} = \\frac{9.8}{\\pi^2} \\approx 0.99 \\text{ m}.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $L = \\dfrac{gT^2}{4\\pi^2}$; a period of 2 seconds requires a string about 0.99 meters long (just under a meter).</strong></p>",
 "S1-6": "<p>(a) $P(200) = 1.25(200) - 40 = 250 - 40 = 210$.<br>(b) $P(32) = 1.25(32) - 40 = 40 - 40 = 0$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (a) $P(200) = 210$ — selling 200 bars earns the store a profit of \\$210. (b) $P(32) = 0$ — selling 32 bars is the break-even point, where revenue exactly covers the store&#x27;s \\$40 cost.</strong></p>",
 "S1-7": "<p>(a) Start with $y = 3 + 2.5m$ and solve for $m$:<br>$$y - 3 = 2.5m \\;\\Rightarrow\\; m = \\frac{y - 3}{2.5} = 0.4y - 1.2, \\qquad C^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{2.5}.$$<br>(b) $C^{-1}(53) = \\dfrac{53 - 3}{2.5} = \\dfrac{50}{2.5} = 20$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $C^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x - 3}{2.5}$ (dollars in, miles out); with \\$53 the class can ride 20 miles.</strong></p>",
 "S1-8": "<p>Set the model equal to 5,000 and solve:<br>$$50 \\cdot 2^{t/3} = 5000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 2^{t/3} = 100 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\frac{t}{3} = \\log_2 100 = \\frac{\\log 100}{\\log 2} \\approx 6.64 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = 3(6.64) \\approx 19.9.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the exact solution is $t = 3\\log_2 100$ hours; the culture reaches 5,000 bacteria after about 19.9 hours.</strong></p>",
 "S1-9": "<p>(a) The first piece is the line $C = 40h$ on $0 &lt; h \\le 3$, rising from near $(0, 0)$ to the junction point $(3, 120)$ (check: $40 \\times 3 = 120$, and the second piece at $h = 3$ gives $120 + 25(0) = 120$, so the graph is continuous there). The second piece starts at $(3, 120)$ with the shallower slope 25, reaching $(8, 120 + 25 \\cdot 5) = (8, 245)$.<br>(b) Since $5 &gt; 3$, use the second piece: $C(5) = 120 + 25(5 - 3) = 120 + 50 = 170$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the graph is two line segments meeting at $(3, \\$120)$; the 5-hour rental costs \\$170.</strong></p>",
 "S1-10": "<p>(a) The negative sign reflects the graph of $f$ across the $x$-axis (the arc opens downward); the $(x - 2)$ shifts the graph 2 units to the right; the $+5$ shifts it 5 units up.<br>(b) The vertex is $(2, 5)$. Since the parabola opens downward, the range is $y \\le 5$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $g$ is $f$ reflected over the $x$-axis, shifted right 2 and up 5; its vertex is $(2, 5)$ with range $y \\le 5$ — the character&#x27;s jump peaks at a height of 5 units, 2 units into the jump.</strong></p>",
 "S1-11": "<p>(a) The height oscillates between $11 - 10 = 1$ m and $11 + 10 = 21$ m with period 40 s, starting at the minimum, so a negative cosine fits:<br>$$h(t) = 11 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{40}t\\right) = 11 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{20}t\\right).$$<br>(b) $h(10) = 11 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 11 - 10(0) = 11$ m.<br>(c) The maximum occurs when $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{20}t\\right) = -1$, i.e., $\\frac{\\pi}{20}t = \\pi$, so $t = 20$ s.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $h(t) = 11 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{20}t\\right)$; after 10 seconds the rider is 11 meters up (level with the center, a quarter-turn through the ride), and the rider first reaches the top at 20 seconds.</strong></p>",
 "S1-12": "<p>The angle $\\angle ACB$ is an inscribed angle intercepting the same arc $AB$ as the central angle $\\angle AOB$. By the inscribed angle theorem, an inscribed angle measures half the measure of its intercepted arc (equivalently, half the central angle on the same arc):<br>$$m\\angle ACB = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(110^\\circ\\right) = 55^\\circ.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the angle between the light beams at the manager&#x27;s seat is $55^\\circ$ — half the central angle, because an inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc.</strong></p>",
 "S1-13": "<p>Group and complete the square for each variable:<br>$$(x^2 - 6x + 9) + (y^2 + 4y + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25.$$<br>So the center is $(3, -2)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ grid units.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the courtyard&#x27;s center is at $(3, -2)$ on the map, and its radius is $5 \\times 10 = 50$ feet.</strong></p>",
 "S1-14": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> Triangle $ABC$ with $BC$ extended to $D$. <strong>Prove:</strong> $m\\angle ACD = m\\angle A + m\\angle B$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statement</th><th>Reason</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $m\\angle A + m\\angle B + m\\angle ACB = 180^\\circ$</td><td>Triangle angle sum theorem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle ACB$ and $\\angle ACD$ form a linear pair on line $BD$</td><td>$BC$ is extended to $D$, so $B$, $C$, $D$ are collinear</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $m\\angle ACB + m\\angle ACD = 180^\\circ$</td><td>Angles in a linear pair are supplementary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $m\\angle A + m\\angle B + m\\angle ACB = m\\angle ACB + m\\angle ACD$</td><td>Transitive property (both sums equal $180^\\circ$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $m\\angle ACD = m\\angle A + m\\angle B$</td><td>Subtraction property of equality (subtract $m\\angle ACB$ from both sides)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the exterior angle at $C$ equals the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles, $m\\angle ACD = m\\angle A + m\\angle B$. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S1-15": "<p>(a) In 25 minutes the hand makes $\\frac{25}{60} = \\frac{5}{12}$ of a full revolution, and a full revolution is $2\\pi$ radians:<br>$$\\theta = \\frac{5}{12}(2\\pi) = \\frac{5\\pi}{6} \\approx 2.62 \\text{ radians}.$$<br>(b) Arc length with $r = 1.2$ m:<br>$$s = r\\theta = 1.2 \\cdot \\frac{5\\pi}{6} = \\pi \\approx 3.14 \\text{ m}.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the hand sweeps $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ radians (about 2.62 rad) in 25 minutes, and its tip travels $\\pi$ meters (about 3.14 m).</strong></p>",
 "S1-16": "<p>The radius is $r = 60 / 2 = 30$ cm. Then<br>$$V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (30)^3 = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (27{,}000) = 36{,}000\\pi \\approx 113{,}097 \\text{ cm}^3.$$<br>Converting: $113{,}097 \\div 1{,}000 \\approx 113.1$ liters.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the beach ball holds $36{,}000\\pi \\approx 113{,}097$ cm³ of air, which is about 113.1 liters.</strong></p>",
 "S1-17": "<p>Floor area: $100 \\times 60 = 6{,}000$ ft².<br>(a) Capacity: $6{,}000 \\div 7.5 = 800$ people.<br>(b) Area per person: $6{,}000 \\div 750 = 8$ ft² per person. Density: $750 \\div 6{,}000 = 0.125$ people per ft².</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (a) at most 800 people may be on the gym floor. (b) With 750 students, each person has 8 square feet, a density of 0.125 people per square foot — under the limit, so the rally complies.</strong></p>",
 "S1-18": "<p>(a) $\\hat{p} = \\dfrac{96}{150} = 0.64$, so 64% of the sample supports a longer lunch.<br>(b) Margin of error $\\approx 2 \\times 0.04 = 0.08$. Interval: $0.64 \\pm 0.08$, i.e., $0.56$ to $0.72$ (56% to 72%).<br>(c) The entire interval lies above 0.50, so even the lowest plausible value is a majority.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\hat{p} = 0.64$ with margin of error about $\\pm 8$ percentage points, giving 56%–72%. Yes, the council can reasonably claim majority support, because the whole interval exceeds 50%.</strong></p>",
 "S1-19": "<p>(1) <strong>Sample survey</strong> — the question asks about opinions/preferences, which are measured by asking a sample of students; no treatment is applied and no behavior is tracked.<br>(2) <strong>Observational study</strong> — students choose for themselves whether to attend tutoring, and the researcher only observes the outcomes. Because attendance is self-selected (motivated students may both attend and earn high grades), the study can show an association but not causation.<br>(3) <strong>Experiment</strong> — the question asks whether the review program <em>causes</em> higher scores, so the program must be imposed as a treatment and compared against a control group. Random assignment of students to the two groups balances other variables (ability, motivation, teacher) between the groups, so a difference in scores can be attributed to the program itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (1) survey, (2) observational study, (3) experiment — and it is random assignment in (3) that makes a cause-and-effect conclusion defensible.</strong></p>",
 "S1-20": "<p>(a) Cylinder sliced parallel to its bases: a <strong>circle</strong> (congruent to the bases). Sliced perpendicular to its bases through the axis: a <strong>rectangle</strong>.<br>(b) Cone sliced parallel to its base: a <strong>circle</strong> (smaller than the base). Sliced through the apex perpendicular to the base: a <strong>triangle</strong> (with the apex as a vertex and a diameter of the base as its base).<br>(c) Any plane slice of a sphere is a <strong>circle</strong>. The slice through the center is larger: its radius equals the sphere&#x27;s full radius (a &quot;great circle&quot;), while an off-center slice has a smaller radius because the cutting plane passes at some distance from the center.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: cylinder → circle and rectangle; cone → circle and triangle; sphere → circles, with the center slice the largest because it uses the sphere&#x27;s full radius.</strong></p>",
 "S2-1": "<p>Factor by grouping:<br>$$x^3 - 4x^2 + 9x - 36 = x^2(x - 4) + 9(x - 4) = (x - 4)(x^2 + 9).$$<br>Set each factor to zero: $x - 4 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 4$; $x^2 + 9 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = -9 \\Rightarrow x = 3i$ or $x = -3i$.<br>There are exactly 3 solutions counting multiplicity, matching the degree of the cubic, as the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra guarantees.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the solutions are $x = 4$, $x = 3i$, and $x = -3i$. Only $x = 4$ can be an actual dial setting, since a dial setting is a real number; the two complex solutions complete the algebraic solution set.</strong></p>",
 "S2-2": "<p>(a) From the factored form, the zeros are $t = 0$, $t = -2$, and $t = 4$. The zero at $t = 4$ means the drone is level with the hilltop again 4 minutes after launch.<br>(b) $P$ is a cubic with positive leading coefficient (leading term $t^3$), so as $t \\to -\\infty$, $P(t) \\to -\\infty$, and as $t \\to \\infty$, $P(t) \\to \\infty$. The zero at $t = -2$ would mean 2 minutes <em>before</em> launch, which is outside the flight&#x27;s domain $t \\ge 0$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: zeros are $-2$, $0$, and $4$; the graph falls to the left and rises to the right. In context, the drone starts at hilltop height ($t = 0$) and returns to it at $t = 4$ minutes; $t = -2$ is not a possible flight time.</strong></p>",
 "S2-3": "<p>Divide using synthetic division with $x - 2$ (so use 2) on the coefficients $1, -3, 5, -4$:<br>bring down 1; multiply by 2 → 2, add to $-3$ → $-1$; multiply by 2 → $-2$, add to 5 → 3; multiply by 2 → 6, add to $-4$ → 2.<br>The quotient is $x^2 - x + 3$ and the remainder is 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\dfrac{W(x)}{x - 2} = x^2 - x + 3 + \\dfrac{2}{x - 2}$, with quotient $x^2 - x + 3$ and remainder 2.</strong></p>",
 "S2-4": "<p>(a) $A(t)$ is a line with intercept 50 and slope 15; $B(t)$ is a line with intercept 110 and slope 5. Drone B starts higher, but Drone A climbs faster, so the lines cross.<br>(b) Solve $A(t) = B(t)$:<br>$$50 + 15t = 110 + 5t \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 10t = 60 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = 6, \\qquad A(6) = 50 + 15(6) = 140.$$<br>The intersection point is $(6, 140)$. Its $t$-coordinate solves $A(t) = B(t)$ because an intersection point lies on both graphs, so its coordinates satisfy both altitude equations at once — the common altitude is exactly a solution of the equation.<br>(c) The drones are at the same altitude at that moment.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the drones meet at an altitude of 140 meters, 6 minutes after launch.</strong></p>",
 "S2-5": "<p>(a) Using $a^2 + b^2 = (a + bi)(a - bi)$:<br>$$x^2 + 16 = (x + 4i)(x - 4i), \\qquad 4x^2 + 25 = (2x + 5i)(2x - 5i).$$<br>(b) Check: $(2x + 5i)(2x - 5i) = 4x^2 - (5i)^2 = 4x^2 - 25i^2 = 4x^2 - 25(-1) = 4x^2 + 25$. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $x^2 + 16 = (x + 4i)(x - 4i)$ and $4x^2 + 25 = (2x + 5i)(2x - 5i)$, confirmed by multiplying back out.</strong></p>",
 "S2-6": "<p>(a) The largest table value is $T(9) = 74$, and $t = 9$ hours after 6:00 AM is 3:00 PM.<br>(b) The values rise from $t = 0$ to $t = 9$ and fall from $t = 9$ to $t = 18$: increasing on $[0, 9]$, decreasing on $[9, 18]$.<br>(c) Noon is $t = 6$: average rate of change $= \\dfrac{T(6) - T(0)}{6 - 0} = \\dfrac{70 - 52}{6} = \\dfrac{18}{6} = 3$ °F per hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the day&#x27;s maximum is 74°F at 3:00 PM; the temperature increases all morning until 3 PM and decreases afterward; from 6:00 AM to noon it warms at an average rate of 3°F per hour.</strong></p>",
 "S2-7": "<p>(a) Tree A starts at $A(0) = 3$ ft; Tree B&#x27;s table gives 4 ft at $t = 0$. Tree B was taller at planting, by 1 foot.<br>(b) Tree A&#x27;s rate is 2.5 ft/year (the coefficient of $t$). Tree B&#x27;s rate from the table: $\\dfrac{9 - 4}{2 - 0} = 2.5$ ft/year. The growth rates are equal.<br>(c) At $t = 10$: $A(10) = 2.5(10) + 3 = 28$ ft; $B(10) = 4 + 2.5(10) = 29$ ft. Tree B is taller, by 1 foot.<br>(d) No. Because both trees grow at the same rate, the 1-foot gap from planting never changes — Tree A can never catch up.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Tree B started 1 ft taller (4 ft vs. 3 ft); both grow at 2.5 ft/year; after 10 years Tree B is still taller, 29 ft to 28 ft; Tree A never catches up because equal rates preserve the initial gap.</strong></p>",
 "S2-8": "<p>(a) Recursive: $a_0 = 40$, $a_n = 1.15\\,a_{n-1}$ for $n \\ge 1$ (each year the flock is 115% of the previous year&#x27;s).<br>(b) Explicit: $a_n = 40(1.15)^n$.<br>(c) $a_3 = 40(1.15)^3 = 40(1.520875) \\approx 60.8$, so about 61 geese. This assumes the 15% growth rate stays constant over the three years.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $a_0 = 40,\\ a_n = 1.15a_{n-1}$; explicitly $a_n = 40(1.15)^n$; after 3 years the refuge can expect roughly 61 geese, assuming the growth rate holds.</strong></p>",
 "S2-9": "<p>(a)</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$t$ (years)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$P(t) = 3t^2 + 10$ (m²)</td><td>10</td><td>13</td><td>22</td><td>37</td><td>58</td><td>85</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$E(t) = 5 \\cdot 2^t$ (m²)</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>80</td><td>160</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(b) At $t = 2$, P leads 22 to 20; at $t = 3$, E leads 40 to 37. So Species E first covers more in year 3.<br>(c) Species E doubles every year, so the amount it <em>adds</em> each year keeps doubling (5, 10, 20, 40, 80, …). Species P&#x27;s yearly additions grow only by a constant step (3, 9, 15, 21, 27, …, increasing by 6 each year). Once E is ahead, its yearly gains outgrow P&#x27;s more and more, so the gap widens without bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Species E first overtakes Species P at $t = 3$ years (40 m² vs. 37 m²), and because exponential doubling outpaces any polynomial growth, E&#x27;s lead keeps growing.</strong></p>",
 "S2-10": "<p>(a) $f(4) = 59$: at 4:00 AM the greenhouse temperature was 59°F.<br>(b) $f^{-1}(66) = 6$: the temperature was 66°F at 6:00 AM. $f^{-1}(71) = 8$: the temperature was 71°F at 8:00 AM. (The inverse takes a temperature as input and returns the time.)<br>(c) The grower is asking for $f^{-1}(59) = 4$ — it was 59°F at 4:00 AM.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f(4) = 59$ (59°F at 4 AM); $f^{-1}(66) = 6$ and $f^{-1}(71) = 8$ (66°F at 6 AM, 71°F at 8 AM); the grower&#x27;s answer is $f^{-1}(59) = 4$, i.e., 4:00 AM.</strong></p>",
 "S2-11": "<p>(a) $\\theta = \\frac{5\\pi}{4}$ lies in Quadrant III (between $\\pi$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$). The unit-circle point is $\\left(\\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{4}, \\sin\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.<br>(b) $\\sin\\theta = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\cos\\theta = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Both are negative because in Quadrant III both the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of the point are negative, and cosine/sine are exactly those coordinates.<br>(c) $\\frac{13\\pi}{6} - 2\\pi = \\frac{13\\pi}{6} - \\frac{12\\pi}{6} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Subtracting a full revolution ($2\\pi$) returns the blade to the same position, so sine and cosine repeat — the trigonometric functions are periodic with period $2\\pi$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: at $\\theta = \\frac{5\\pi}{4}$ the tip is in Quadrant III at $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$, with $\\sin\\theta = \\cos\\theta = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$; the angle $\\frac{13\\pi}{6}$ is the same position as $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, by $2\\pi$-periodicity.</strong></p>",
 "S2-12": "<p>(a) $72^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ radians.<br>(b) Arc length: $s = r\\theta = 25 \\cdot \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} = 10\\pi \\approx 31.4$ m.<br>(c) Sector area: $A = \\tfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = \\tfrac{1}{2}(25)^2\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right) = 125\\pi \\approx 392.7$ m².</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $72^\\circ = \\frac{2\\pi}{5}$ radians; the curved path is $10\\pi \\approx 31.4$ meters long, and the lawn wedge has area $125\\pi \\approx 392.7$ square meters.</strong></p>",
 "S2-13": "<p>(a) The focus $(0, 2)$ and directrix $y = -2$ put the vertex at the origin with $p = 2$ (the focus is 2 units above the vertex). The parabola opens upward, so $x^2 = 4py = 4(2)y = 8y$.<br>(Equivalently, setting the distance from $(x, y)$ to $(0, 2)$ equal to its distance to the line $y = -2$ and simplifying gives the same equation.)<br>(b) At $x = 4$: $16 = 8y \\Rightarrow y = 2$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the cross-section is $x^2 = 8y$; at $x = 4$ inches from the center the dish is 2 inches deep.</strong></p>",
 "S2-14": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> $ABCD$ is a parallelogram with diagonal $\\overline{AC}$. <strong>Prove:</strong> $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statement</th><th>Reason</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{CB}$</td><td>Definition of a parallelogram (both pairs of opposite sides are parallel)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle BAC \\cong \\angle DCA$</td><td>Alternate interior angles are congruent (parallel lines $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ cut by transversal $\\overline{AC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\angle DAC \\cong \\angle BCA$</td><td>Alternate interior angles are congruent (parallel lines $\\overline{AD}$ and $\\overline{CB}$ cut by transversal $\\overline{AC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td><td>Reflexive property</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle CDA$</td><td>ASA congruence (statements 2, 4, 3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{CB}$</td><td>CPCTC — corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Final answer: drawing diagonal $\\overline{AC}$ splits the parallelogram into two congruent triangles (ASA), so the opposite sides, as corresponding parts, are congruent. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S2-15": "<p>(a) Midline $= 70$°F — the average temperature around which the day oscillates. Amplitude $= 8$°F — the temperature swings 8°F above and below the midline (from 62°F to 78°F). Period: $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi/12} = 24$ hours — the daily cycle.<br>(b) The maximum occurs when the sine equals 1: $\\frac{\\pi}{12}(t - 9) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\Rightarrow t - 9 = 6 \\Rightarrow t = 15$, i.e., 3:00 PM, with $T = 70 + 8 = 78$°F.<br>(c) 6:00 PM is $t = 18$: $T(18) = 70 + 8\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}(9)\\right) = 70 + 8\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = 70 + 8\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = 70 + 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 75.7$°F.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: midline 70°F, amplitude 8°F, period 24 hours; the day peaks at 78°F at 3:00 PM; at 6:00 PM the temperature is about 75.7°F.</strong></p>",
 "S2-16": "<p>(a) $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi (3)^2 (4) = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi (36) = 12\\pi \\approx 37.70$ ft³.<br>(b) $37.70 \\div 27 \\approx 1.396$, so about 1.40 yd³.<br>(c) Since $1.40 &lt; 1.5$, ordering 1.5 cubic yards covers the pile&#x27;s volume, with a little to spare.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the pile is $12\\pi \\approx 37.70$ cubic feet, about 1.40 cubic yards — so yes, a 1.5-cubic-yard order is enough.</strong></p>",
 "S2-17": "<p>Cylinder: $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (3)^2 (8) = 72\\pi$ m³.<br>Hemispherical top: $V = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi (3)^3 = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi (27) = 18\\pi$ m³.<br>Total: $72\\pi + 18\\pi = 90\\pi \\approx 282.7$ m³.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: modeled as a cylinder plus a hemisphere, the silo holds $90\\pi \\approx 282.7$ cubic meters of grain.</strong></p>",
 "S2-18": "<p>(a) Observed difference of means: $18.2 - 15.6 = 2.6$ cm, in favor of Treatment A.<br>(b) Proportion of simulations with a difference of 2.6 cm or more: $\\dfrac{4}{200} = 0.02$, or 2%.<br>(c) A difference this large occurred in only 2% of random reassignments when there was no treatment effect — too rare to attribute plausibly to chance. The difference is statistically significant.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the observed difference is 2.6 cm favoring Treatment A; only 2% of the chance simulations matched or beat it, so the difference is statistically significant — the experiment gives real evidence that Treatment A produces taller tomato plants than Treatment B.</strong></p>",
 "S2-19": "<p>Problems with the poll:<br>1. <strong>Voluntary, self-selected sample.</strong> Only readers who chose to click answered. People with strong feelings about the park are far more likely to respond, so the 68% reflects motivated clickers, not residents in general.<br>2. <strong>Wrong population.</strong> The sample is drawn from visitors to one news site — who may not even live in the area — while the headline makes a claim about <em>all residents</em>. The sample frame does not match the population.<br>3. <strong>The margin of error is not valid.</strong> A margin of error of ±3 points comes from formulas that assume a <em>random</em> sample, where every resident had a known chance of selection. With a voluntary poll there is no random selection, so no such margin of error applies. (Independently, even a random sample of only 214 people would carry a margin of error of roughly ±7 points, not ±3.)<br>4. Additional issues: nothing prevents multiple votes or outside organizing to flood the poll, and nonresponse is total for everyone who never saw the link.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the 68% figure describes 214 self-selected website readers, not the town&#x27;s residents, and the claimed ±3-point margin of error is meaningless because the poll used no random sampling. The headline&#x27;s claim about &quot;residents&quot; is unsupported.</strong></p>",
 "S2-20": "<p>(a) The two perpendicular sides use $2x$ feet of fencing, so the side along the barn gets $60 - 2x$ feet. Area:<br>$$A(x) = x(60 - 2x) = -2x^2 + 60x.$$<br>(b) $A$ is a downward-opening parabola. Its vertex is at $x = \\dfrac{-60}{2(-2)} = 15$. Then the barn side is $60 - 2(15) = 30$ feet, and the maximum area is $A(15) = 15 \\times 30 = 450$ ft².</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: with the perpendicular sides 15 feet and the side parallel to the barn 30 feet, the garden reaches its maximum area of 450 square feet.</strong></p>",
 "S3-1": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, $1 - i$:</p>\n<p>$$\\frac{8 + 6i}{1 + i} \\cdot \\frac{1 - i}{1 - i} = \\frac{(8 + 6i)(1 - i)}{(1 + i)(1 - i)}$$</p>\n<p>Numerator: $(8 + 6i)(1 - i) = 8 - 8i + 6i - 6i^2 = 8 - 2i + 6 = 14 - 2i$ (using $i^2 = -1$).<br>Denominator: $(1 + i)(1 - i) = 1 - i^2 = 1 + 1 = 2$.</p>\n<p>$$\\frac{14 - 2i}{2} = 7 - i$$</p>\n<p>The conjugate works because a complex number times its conjugate is always a real number ($a^2 + b^2$), which turns the denominator into an ordinary real number that can divide each part.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the calibration value is $7 - i$ ohms.</strong></p>",
 "S3-2": "<p>(a) The term $210x$: since $x$ counts cars, $210x$ represents money coming in proportional to the number of cars — effectively $\\$210$ of gross margin per car detailed (price minus per-car supplies), before fixed costs and overtime effects.</p>\n<p>(b) The constant $-1200$ is the weekly fixed cost: $\\$1{,}200$ the business owes each week (van payment, insurance, booth rent) even if it details zero cars — which is why $P(0) = -1200$.</p>\n<p>(c) A negative <em>linear</em> term would subtract the same amount for every additional car — a constant per-car cost — so profit per car would never change. The term $-3x^2$ grows faster as $x$ grows: going from 10 to 11 cars subtracts $3(11^2 - 10^2) = 3(21) = \\$63$ of &quot;extra cost,&quot; while going from 30 to 31 cars subtracts $3(31^2 - 30^2) = 3(61) = \\$183$. The marginal cost accelerates, which is exactly the overtime behavior the owner describes: each extra car is more expensive to serve than the last.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $210x$ is the per-car gross margin (about \\$210 per car), $-1200$ is the \\$1,200 weekly fixed cost, and $-3x^2$ models accelerating overtime costs because its per-car effect grows with $x$.</strong></p>",
 "S3-3": "<p>Solve $\\sqrt{x + 7} = x - 5$. Note first that the right side must be nonnegative, so any valid solution needs $x \\ge 5$.</p>\n<p>Square both sides:<br>$$x + 7 = (x - 5)^2 = x^2 - 10x + 25$$<br>$$0 = x^2 - 11x + 18 = (x - 9)(x - 2)$$<br>Candidates: $x = 9$ and $x = 2$.</p>\n<p>Check in the <strong>original</strong> equation:<br>• $x = 9$: $\\sqrt{9 + 7} = \\sqrt{16} = 4$ and $9 - 5 = 4$. ✓ Works.<br>• $x = 2$: $\\sqrt{2 + 7} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ but $2 - 5 = -3$. ✗ $3 \\ne -3$ — extraneous (it also violates the requirement $x \\ge 5$). Squaring erased the sign difference between $3$ and $-3$, which is how this false solution appeared.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the brace must be $x = 9$ inches; $x = 2$ is an extraneous solution introduced by squaring and cannot be used.</strong></p>",
 "S3-4": "<p>Factor everything:<br>$$\\frac{x^2 - 9}{x^2 + 5x} \\div \\frac{x - 3}{x^2 + 10x + 25} = \\frac{(x - 3)(x + 3)}{x(x + 5)} \\div \\frac{x - 3}{(x + 5)^2}$$</p>\n<p>Rewrite division as multiplication by the reciprocal and cancel common factors:<br>$$\\frac{(x - 3)(x + 3)}{x(x + 5)} \\cdot \\frac{(x + 5)^2}{x - 3} = \\frac{(x + 3)(x + 5)}{x}$$</p>\n<p>Excluded values come from every denominator in the original expression and from the numerator of the divisor (which becomes a denominator after flipping): $x(x + 5) = 0$ gives $x = 0, -5$; $(x + 5)^2 = 0$ gives $x = -5$; the divisor&#x27;s numerator $x - 3 = 0$ gives $x = 3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the simplified yield factor is $\\dfrac{(x + 3)(x + 5)}{x}$, with excluded values $x = -5, 0, 3$ (the problem&#x27;s condition $x &gt; 3$ avoids all of them).</strong></p>",
 "S3-5": "<p>(a) Solve $A = P(1 + rt)$ for $r$:<br>$$\\frac{A}{P} = 1 + rt \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\frac{A}{P} - 1 = rt \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad r = \\frac{A - P}{Pt}$$</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute $A = 5400$, $P = 5000$, $t = 2$:<br>$$r = \\frac{5400 - 5000}{5000 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{400}{10000} = 0.04$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the certificate&#x27;s annual interest rate is $0.04$, i.e., 4% per year.</strong></p>",
 "S3-6": "<p>(a) The input $t$ runs from opening ($t = 0$, 6:00 AM) to closing ($t = 8$, 2:00 PM), so the domain is $0 \\le t \\le 8$ hours. The output $C$ runs from a low of 0 customers (at closing) to the peak of 48, so the range is $0 \\le C \\le 48$ customers.</p>\n<p>(b) The bakery is busiest at $t = 3$ (9:00 AM), where the graph reaches its maximum value of 48 customers — the peak point $(3, 48)$ is the highest point on the graph.</p>\n<p>(c) $C$ is a function of $t$ because at each instant $t$ the bakery contains exactly one definite number of customers; no time corresponds to two different customer counts, so each input has exactly one output (equivalently, the graph passes the vertical line test).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: domain $0 \\le t \\le 8$ (6:00 AM–2:00 PM), range $0 \\le C \\le 48$ customers, busiest at 9:00 AM with 48 customers, and $C$ is a function because each time has exactly one customer count.</strong></p>",
 "S3-7": "<p>(a) The constraints are:<br>$$x + y \\le 240 \\quad\\text{(item capacity)}$$<br>$$3x + 2y \\le 600 \\quad\\text{(grill minutes)}$$<br>$$x \\ge 0, \\qquad y \\ge 0$$</p>\n<p>(b) Test $(100, 140)$: items: $100 + 140 = 240 \\le 240$ ✓. Grill time: $3(100) + 2(140) = 300 + 280 = 580 \\le 600$ ✓. Both counts are nonnegative ✓.</p>\n<p>(c) The constraints $x \\ge 0$, $y \\ge 0$ restrict the domain of the model to counts that can physically exist. They belong in the system because the other two inequalities alone would admit meaningless &quot;solutions&quot; like $x = -50$, $y = 290$ (which satisfies $x + y \\le 240$ and $3x + 2y = 430 \\le 600$) — plans the stand could never carry out.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the system is $x + y \\le 240$, $3x + 2y \\le 600$, $x \\ge 0$, $y \\ge 0$; the manager&#x27;s plan of 100 hot dogs and 140 pretzels is viable — it uses exactly 240 items and 580 of the 600 grill minutes.</strong></p>",
 "S3-8": "<p>(a) $P(x) = R(x) - C(x) = 2.50x - (0.60x + 12) = 1.90x - 12$.</p>\n<p>(b) $P(40) = 1.90(40) - 12 = 76 - 12 = 64$.</p>\n<p>(c) The coefficient $1.90$ is the net earnings per mile: the courier takes in $\\$2.50$ per mile but spends $\\$0.60$ per mile, keeping $\\$1.90$ per mile. The constant $-12$ is the fixed $\\$12$ daily dispatch fee, owed even on a zero-mile day (which is why $P(0) = -12$).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $P(x) = 1.90x - 12$ dollars; on a 40-mile day the courier&#x27;s profit is $\\$64$.</strong></p>",
 "S3-9": "<p>Compute both compositions.</p>\n<p>$f(g(x)) = 2\\left(\\dfrac{x - 6}{2}\\right) + 6 = (x - 6) + 6 = x$. ✓</p>\n<p>$g(f(x)) = \\dfrac{(2x + 6) - 6}{2} = \\dfrac{2x}{2} = x$. ✓</p>\n<p>Both compositions must be checked because &quot;undoing&quot; has to work in both directions: $f(g(x)) = x$ shows $g$ reverses $f$ when applied first on the outside, and $g(f(x)) = x$ shows starting from a base cost, applying the shop&#x27;s pricing and then the bookkeeper&#x27;s recovery returns the original base cost. For functions whose domains are restricted, one composition can equal $x$ while the other does not, so a single check is not a proof.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f(g(x)) = x$ and $g(f(x)) = x$, so the bookkeeper is right — $g$ is the inverse of $f$: it recovers the base cost from any final price.</strong></p>",
 "S3-10": "<p>(a) The graph of $F(t) = 300(2)^t$ starts at the $F$-intercept $(0, 300)$, rises increasingly steeply (doubling each month), and has the horizontal asymptote $F = 0$ on the left (as $t \\to -\\infty$, $F \\to 0$); the curve never touches or crosses $F = 0$.</p>\n<p>(b) End behavior: as $t \\to \\infty$, $F(t) \\to \\infty$. In context, the model predicts the following grows without bound — realistic only in the short run, since a real audience is finite; the model will eventually overstate growth.</p>\n<p>(c) $G(t) = 300\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)^t$ has the same intercept $(0, 300)$ and the same horizontal asymptote $F = 0$, but because the base $\\tfrac12$ is between 0 and 1, $G$ <em>decreases</em>: as $t \\to \\infty$, $G(t) \\to 0$ (the following halves each month and fades toward zero), while as $t \\to -\\infty$ it grows. The two graphs are mirror images of each other across the vertical line through the intercept direction — $F$ climbs away from the asymptote, $G$ decays toward it.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $F$ starts at 300 followers, doubles monthly, and grows without bound with asymptote $F = 0$; $G$ starts at the same 300 but decays toward 0 followers with the same asymptote.</strong></p>",
 "S3-11": "<p>Draw the radius from the center $O$ perpendicular to the chord (the bar), meeting the bar at point $M$. By the theorem that a radius (or line from the center) perpendicular to a chord bisects the chord, $M$ is the midpoint of the bar, so each half has length $\\tfrac{1}{2}(\\text{bar})$.</p>\n<p>The radius to either end of the bar, the perpendicular segment $OM = 5$ in., and half the bar form a right triangle with hypotenuse 13 in. (the radius). By the Pythagorean theorem:<br>$$\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{bar}\\right)^2 + 5^2 = 13^2 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{bar}\\right)^2 = 169 - 25 = 144 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\tfrac{1}{2}\\text{bar} = 12 \\text{ in.}$$</p>\n<p>Therefore the full bar is $2 \\times 12 = 24$ inches.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the support bar is 24 inches long (a 5-12-13 right triangle hides in the sign: half the bar is 12 inches).</strong></p>",
 "S3-12": "<p>(a) By the <em>definition</em> of radian measure, an angle&#x27;s measure in radians is the length of the arc it cuts on a unit circle. The display&#x27;s radius is 1 foot, so it <em>is</em> a unit circle (scaled to feet): an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$ radians subtends an arc of exactly $\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$ feet.</p>\n<p>(b) $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4} \\approx 5.50$ feet.</p>\n<p>(c) One full turn is $2\\pi$ radians and a right angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, so a quarter of a right angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ radians. &quot;One full turn minus a quarter of a right angle&quot; is $2\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} = \\dfrac{8\\pi}{4} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$ radians — exactly the given rotation. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the necklace travels $\\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\approx 5.50$ feet along its arc, and the catalog&#x27;s description is correct.</strong></p>",
 "S3-13": "<p>(a) Opposite angles of an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary, so:<br>$$(3x + 10) + (5x - 30) = 180 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 8x - 20 = 180 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 8x = 200 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 25$$</p>\n<p>(b) $A = 3(25) + 10 = 85^\\circ$ and $C = 5(25) - 30 = 95^\\circ$. (Check: $85 + 95 = 180$. ✓)</p>\n<p>(c) Theorem: opposite angles of a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle are supplementary. It holds because each inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc; angles $A$ and $C$ intercept arcs that together make up the entire circle ($360^\\circ$), so $A + C = \\tfrac12(360^\\circ) = 180^\\circ$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $x = 25$, with $A = 85^\\circ$ and $C = 95^\\circ$ — supplementary, as the inscribed-quadrilateral theorem requires.</strong></p>",
 "S3-14": "<p>(a) A point $(x, y)$ is on the boundary exactly when its distance from the depot $(2, -3)$ equals 6 miles. By the distance formula (the Pythagorean theorem applied to the horizontal and vertical legs):<br>$$\\sqrt{(x - 2)^2 + (y - (-3))^2} = 6$$<br>Squaring both sides:<br>$$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 36$$</p>\n<p>(b) Test $(6, 1)$: $(6 - 2)^2 + (1 + 3)^2 = 16 + 16 = 32$. Since $32 &lt; 36$, the point lies <em>inside</em> the circle (its distance from the depot is $\\sqrt{32} \\approx 5.66$ miles, less than 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the zone&#x27;s boundary is $(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 36$; the customer at $(6, 1)$ is inside the guaranteed delivery zone.</strong></p>",
 "S3-15": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> $\\triangle ABC$ with $A(0, 0)$, $B(8, 0)$, $C(2, 6)$; $D$ is the midpoint of $AB$ and $E$ is the midpoint of $AC$. <strong>Prove:</strong> $DE \\parallel BC$ and $DE = \\tfrac{1}{2}BC$.</p>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Find the midpoints (midpoint formula).</em> <br>$D = \\left(\\frac{0 + 8}{2}, \\frac{0 + 0}{2}\\right) = (4, 0)$; $\\quad E = \\left(\\frac{0 + 2}{2}, \\frac{0 + 6}{2}\\right) = (1, 3)$.</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Compare slopes (two lines with equal slopes are parallel).</em> <br>Slope of $DE$: $\\dfrac{3 - 0}{1 - 4} = \\dfrac{3}{-3} = -1$. <br>Slope of $BC$: $\\dfrac{6 - 0}{2 - 8} = \\dfrac{6}{-6} = -1$. <br>The slopes are equal, so $DE \\parallel BC$. ∎ (first claim)</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Compare lengths (distance formula).</em> <br>$DE = \\sqrt{(1 - 4)^2 + (3 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 9} = \\sqrt{18} = 3\\sqrt{2}$ ft. <br>$BC = \\sqrt{(2 - 8)^2 + (6 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 36} = \\sqrt{72} = 6\\sqrt{2}$ ft. <br>Since $3\\sqrt{2} = \\tfrac{1}{2}(6\\sqrt{2})$, $DE = \\tfrac{1}{2}BC$. ∎ (second claim)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: both claims are proven — the walkway is parallel to side $BC$ (equal slopes of $-1$) and is $3\\sqrt{2} \\approx 4.24$ feet long, exactly half of $BC$&#x27;s $6\\sqrt{2} \\approx 8.49$ feet, so the architect can order materials with confidence.</strong></p>",
 "S3-16": "<p>(a) $V = \\frac{1}{3}Bh = \\frac{1}{3}(6 \\cdot 6)(4) = \\frac{1}{3}(36)(4) = 48$ cubic inches.</p>\n<p>(b) $480 \\div 48 = 10$ candles exactly.</p>\n<p>(c) A prism with the same base and height has volume $Bh = 144$ in³. The pyramid&#x27;s volume is exactly one-third of that prism&#x27;s volume ($48 = \\tfrac{1}{3} \\cdot 144$): the factor $\\tfrac{1}{3}$ expresses that three identical pyramids&#x27; worth of wax fills the matching prism — the pyramid holds a third as much as the box-shaped solid on the same base with the same height.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: each candle uses 48 in³ of wax, so one 480 in³ pot pours exactly 10 candles.</strong></p>",
 "S3-17": "<p>(a) Density $= \\dfrac{\\text{mass}}{\\text{volume}} = \\dfrac{1925 \\text{ g}}{250 \\text{ cm}^3} = 7.7\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$.</p>\n<p>(b) $7.7\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$ is closest to iron&#x27;s $7.9\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$ (far from aluminum&#x27;s 2.7 and lead&#x27;s 11.3), so the block is most likely iron (or a similar ferrous alloy). The assumption is that the block is a single, uniform, solid material with no hollow spaces or mixed composition, and that the measured density&#x27;s small gap from 7.9 is measurement/alloy variation.</p>\n<p>(c) Same density: volume $= \\dfrac{\\text{mass}}{\\text{density}} = \\dfrac{3850}{7.7} = 500\\ \\text{cm}^3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the metal&#x27;s density is $7.7\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$, most consistent with iron; a 3,850 g block of it would occupy 500 cm³.</strong></p>",
 "S3-18": "<p>(a) Population: all residents of the town. Sample: the 150 randomly selected residents who were surveyed (96 of whom, i.e., 64% of the sample, support the market).</p>\n<p>(b) Random selection gives every resident a comparable chance of being included, so the sample tends to mirror the town&#x27;s mix of opinions. Surveying people at the existing Saturday market would massively overrepresent people who already shop at markets and like them — supporters would be far more common in the sample than in the town — so the 64%-style estimate would be biased upward and could not be trusted as a statement about all residents.</p>\n<p>(c) It is an inference because 64% was measured only on the 150-person sample; the claim extends that number to thousands of un-surveyed residents, which involves uncertainty the measurement itself doesn&#x27;t have. The study is a <strong>sample survey</strong> (it asks opinions; it imposes no treatment and does not merely observe an existing condition in a records-based observational design).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: population = all town residents, sample = the 150 randomly chosen residents; random selection is what licenses the estimate &quot;about 64% of residents support the market&quot; as an inference about the whole town, and the study is a sample survey.</strong></p>",
 "S3-19": "<p>(a) Margin of error $\\approx 2 \\times 0.5 = 1.0$ minute.</p>\n<p>(b) Interval: $4.6 \\pm 1.0$, i.e., from $3.6$ to $5.6$ minutes.</p>\n<p>(c) The claim &quot;average wait under 4 minutes&quot; is <strong>not established</strong> by this data. Values below 4 minutes are inside the interval, but so are values well above 4 (up to 5.6 minutes), so the true mean could plausibly be 5 minutes. Because the interval does not lie entirely below 4, the owner cannot honestly advertise the claim on this evidence — she would need a larger sample (which shrinks the margin of error) to settle it.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: margin of error ≈ 1.0 minute; the true mean wait is plausibly between 3.6 and 5.6 minutes, so the &quot;under 4 minutes&quot; claim is possible but not supported.</strong></p>",
 "S3-20": "<p>(a) Rotating a rectangle about one of its sides sweeps out a <strong>cylinder</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) The side on the axis (length 10 cm) becomes the cylinder&#x27;s <strong>height</strong>: $h = 10$ cm. The side perpendicular to the axis (width 4 cm) sweeps around and becomes the <strong>radius</strong>: $r = 4$ cm, because every point of the outer edge stays exactly 4 cm from the axis.</p>\n<p>(c) The trainee is wrong. Rotating about the short side puts the 4 cm side on the axis and the 10 cm side sweeping around, generating a cylinder with height $4$ cm and radius $10$ cm — a short, wide disk-like cylinder, not the tall, narrow one from part (b). The two solids have different radii and heights (and different volumes: $\\pi(4^2)(10) = 160\\pi$ cm³ vs. $\\pi(10^2)(4) = 400\\pi$ cm³), so they are not the same cylinder in a different position.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: rotation about the long side generates a cylinder with radius 4 cm and height 10 cm; rotation about the short side generates a different cylinder with radius 10 cm and height 4 cm.</strong></p>",
 "S4-1": "<p>(a) Treat $x^2 + 49$ as a difference of squares in the complex numbers: since $49 = -(7i)^2$ (because $(7i)^2 = 49i^2 = -49$),<br>$$x^2 + 49 = x^2 - (7i)^2 = (x + 7i)(x - 7i)$$</p>\n<p>(b) Expand to verify:<br>$$(x + 7i)(x - 7i) = x^2 - 7ix + 7ix - 49i^2 = x^2 - 49(-1) = x^2 + 49 \\checkmark$$</p>\n<p>(c) The graph of $y = x^2 + 49$ is a parabola with vertex $(0, 49)$ opening upward, so it lies entirely above the $x$-axis and has no $x$-intercepts — hence no real zeros, and therefore no factorization into real linear factors. The complex factors correspond to the complex zeros $x = \\pm 7i$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $x^2 + 49 = (x + 7i)(x - 7i)$; real factorization is impossible because the parabola never crosses the $x$-axis.</strong></p>",
 "S4-2": "<p>(a) $P(x) = (x - 2)^2(x + 5)$ is already factored. Zeros: $x = 2$ with multiplicity 2 (from the squared factor), and $x = -5$ with multiplicity 1.</p>\n<p>(b) $P$ is a cubic (degree 3) with positive leading coefficient, so as $x \\to -\\infty$, $P(x) \\to -\\infty$, and as $x \\to \\infty$, $P(x) \\to \\infty$.</p>\n<p>(c) At $x = -5$ (odd multiplicity 1) the graph <strong>crosses</strong> the $x$-axis. At $x = 2$ (even multiplicity 2) the graph <strong>touches</strong> the axis and turns around without crossing — the sign of $P$ is the same on both sides of $x = 2$. Sketch: the curve rises from $-\\infty$, crosses at $(-5, 0)$, rises to a local max, comes back down to touch the axis at $(2, 0)$, then rises to $+\\infty$. (Sign check: $P(0) = (4)(5) = 20 &gt; 0$ and $P(3) = (1)(8) = 8 &gt; 0$, consistent with touching rather than crossing at 2.)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: zeros are $-5$ (multiplicity 1, graph crosses) and $2$ (multiplicity 2, graph touches and turns); the graph falls to the left and rises to the right.</strong></p>",
 "S4-3": "<p>(a) Set the models equal: $40t + 200 = 25t + 500 \\Rightarrow 15t = 300 \\Rightarrow t = 20$. Then $A(20) = 40(20) + 200 = 1000$. The graphs intersect at $(20, 1000)$.</p>\n<p>(b) The intersection means that <strong>20 days</strong> into the campaign, the two sites have given the <strong>same cumulative total of 1,000 doses</strong> each. Site B started ahead (500 vs. 200 doses already recorded at day 0), and day 20 is when Site A&#x27;s total catches up.</p>\n<p>(c) The coordinator&#x27;s statement is wrong: the intersection is about equal <em>totals</em>, not equal <em>rates</em>. Rates are the <strong>slopes</strong> of the graphs: Site A administers 40 doses per day and Site B 25 doses per day, at every moment of the model — those rates are never equal. After day 20, Site A&#x27;s cumulative total pulls ahead precisely because its rate (slope) is larger.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the sites&#x27; cumulative totals are equal (1,000 doses each) at day 20; their rates, 40 vs. 25 doses/day, are the slopes and are never equal.</strong></p>",
 "S4-4": "<p>Divide $3x^2 + 5x - 7$ by $x + 2$:<br>• $3x^2 \\div x = 3x$; multiply: $3x(x + 2) = 3x^2 + 6x$; subtract: $(3x^2 + 5x) - (3x^2 + 6x) = -x$; bring down $-7$.<br>• $-x \\div x = -1$; multiply: $-1(x + 2) = -x - 2$; subtract: $(-x - 7) - (-x - 2) = -5$.</p>\n<p>So $3x^2 + 5x - 7 = (x + 2)(3x - 1) - 5$, and<br>$$\\frac{3x^2 + 5x - 7}{x + 2} = 3x - 1 - \\frac{5}{x + 2}$$<br>(Check: $(x + 2)(3x - 1) = 3x^2 + 5x - 2$; minus 5 gives $3x^2 + 5x - 7$. ✓)</p>\n<p>Interpretation: the average cost per patient behaves like $3x - 1$ dollars with a correction term $-\\dfrac{5}{x + 2}$. The remainder $-5$ is what&#x27;s left undistributed after the division; in the model its effect is a small downward adjustment to the average cost that shrinks toward zero as the session size $x$ grows — the per-patient average settles toward the $3x - 1$ pattern for large sessions.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\dfrac{3x^2 + 5x - 7}{x + 2} = 3x - 1 - \\dfrac{5}{x + 2}$; the remainder $-5$ produces a correction to average cost that fades as more patients are seen.</strong></p>",
 "S4-5": "<p>$$(3 + 2i)(1 - 4i) = 3(1) + 3(-4i) + 2i(1) + 2i(-4i) = 3 - 12i + 2i - 8i^2$$<br>The last term uses $i^2 = -1$: $-8i^2 = -8(-1) = +8$. Combining:<br>$$3 + 8 + (-12i + 2i) = 11 - 10i$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the combined stage value is $11 - 10i$.</strong></p>",
 "S4-6": "<p>(a) Set the model equal to 10,000: $500(1.8)^t = 10000$.</p>\n<p>(b) Divide by 500: $(1.8)^t = 20$. Express the solution as a logarithm:<br>$$t = \\frac{\\log 20}{\\log 1.8}$$<br>Evaluating with technology: $t = \\dfrac{1.30103}{0.25527} \\approx 5.10$.</p>\n<p>(c) The test must be run at about <strong>5.1 hours</strong> after the culture was started. The exact form $t = \\frac{\\log 20}{\\log 1.8}$ is still useful because it is the <em>exact</em> answer — it carries no rounding error, shows precisely which quantities determine the time (the growth factor 1.8 and the 20-fold growth), and can be re-evaluated to any precision; the decimal 5.1 is its practical approximation for scheduling.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the culture reaches 10,000 colonies at $t = \\frac{\\log 20}{\\log 1.8} \\approx 5.1$ hours.</strong></p>",
 "S4-7": "<p>(a) $g(x) = f(2x) + 3 = (2x)^2 + 3 = 4x^2 + 3$.</p>\n<p>(b) The $+3$ outside shifts the graph <strong>up 3 units</strong> (every reach value increases by 3 cm). The $2x$ inside <strong>compresses the graph horizontally by a factor of $\\tfrac12$</strong>: the patient now reaches at week $x$ the value the old model predicted at week $2x$ — progress happens twice as fast along the time axis. (It is a compression, not a stretch, because inputs are effectively doubled.)</p>\n<p>(c) $g(2) = 4(2)^2 + 3 = 16 + 3 = 19$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $g(x) = 4x^2 + 3$ (horizontal compression by $\\tfrac12$, shift up 3); after 2 weeks on the new program the patient&#x27;s reach is 19 cm beyond the starting point.</strong></p>",
 "S4-8": "<p>(a) Zeros from the factored form: $x = -1, 3, 4$. The $y$-intercept: $f(0) = (1)(-3)(-4) = 12$.</p>\n<p>(b) $f$ is a cubic with positive leading coefficient: as $x \\to -\\infty$, $f(x) \\to -\\infty$; as $x \\to \\infty$, $f(x) \\to \\infty$.</p>\n<p>(c) Sign analysis on the intervals cut by the zeros:<br>• $x &lt; -1$: $(-)(-)(-) = $ negative<br>• $-1 &lt; x &lt; 3$: $(+)(-)(-) = $ positive<br>• $3 &lt; x &lt; 4$: $(+)(+)(-) = $ negative<br>• $x &gt; 4$: $(+)(+)(+) = $ positive</p>\n<p>Sketch: the curve comes up from $-\\infty$, crosses the axis at $(-1, 0)$, rises through the $y$-intercept $(0, 12)$ to a local maximum, descends to cross at $(3, 0)$, dips below the axis to a local minimum, crosses back up at $(4, 0)$, and rises to $+\\infty$. In context, the waiting count&#x27;s net change is negative in the first and third intervals and positive in the second and fourth.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: zeros $-1, 3, 4$; $y$-intercept 12; the graph rises from $-\\infty$, crosses at each zero with sign pattern $-, +, -, +$, and exits to $+\\infty$.</strong></p>",
 "S4-9": "<p>(a) Solve $y = x^2 + 2$ for $x$ with $x \\ge 0$: $x = \\sqrt{y - 2}$. Swapping variables, $f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x - 2}$, with domain $x \\ge 2$ (the range of $f$).</p>\n<p>(b) On the full parabola, $f$ is not one-to-one: $x = 2$ and $x = -2$ both give $f(x) = 6$, so no inverse could decide which input produced 6 (the graph fails the horizontal line test). Restricting to $x \\ge 0$ keeps only the right half of the parabola, which is one-to-one, so an inverse exists. (Physically, a sample width is nonnegative anyway.)</p>\n<p>(c) $f^{-1}(11) = \\sqrt{11 - 2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x - 2}$ for $x \\ge 2$; an 11 mL reagent requirement corresponds to a sample width of 3 mm.</strong></p>",
 "S4-10": "<p>(a) $T(3) = -0.2(3)^2 + 1.2(3) + 98.6 = -1.8 + 3.6 + 98.6 = 100.4$.</p>\n<p>(b) &quot;$T(3) = 100.4$&quot; means: 3 hours after the fever began (the input, in hours), the patient&#x27;s temperature is 100.4 degrees Fahrenheit (the output, in °F).</p>\n<p>(c) The constant term, $98.6$, is the value at $h = 0$ — the patient&#x27;s temperature at the onset time in the model — so it answers the question with no computation: the model starts at a normal $98.6^\\circ$F. It is the initial value (the $T$-intercept of the graph).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $T(3) = 100.4$, i.e., the patient&#x27;s temperature 3 hours after onset is $100.4^\\circ$F; the constant 98.6 is the model&#x27;s starting temperature.</strong></p>",
 "S4-11": "<p>(a) Midline: $2.4$ L. Amplitude: $0.5$ L. Period: the coefficient of $t$ is $\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$, so the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2\\pi/5} = 5$ seconds.</p>\n<p>(b) Midline 2.4 L: the resting (baseline) air volume around which the lungs&#x27; volume oscillates — the volume the patient returns to between breaths. Amplitude 0.5 L: the volume of air moved in each breath (the tidal volume) — lung volume swings 0.5 L above and below the baseline. Period 5 s: one complete breath cycle (inhale + exhale) takes 5 seconds.</p>\n<p>(c) Breathing rate: $\\dfrac{60 \\text{ s}}{5 \\text{ s/breath}} = 12$ breaths per minute. Maximum volume: $2.4 + 0.5 = 2.9$ L; minimum volume: $2.4 - 0.5 = 1.9$ L.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: midline 2.4 L, amplitude 0.5 L, period 5 s — a resting volume of 2.4 L, breaths of 0.5 L, at 12 breaths per minute, with lung volume ranging from 1.9 L to 2.9 L.</strong></p>",
 "S4-12": "<p>(a) $A = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}(80)^2(1.2) = \\frac{1}{2}(6400)(1.2) = 3840\\ \\text{cm}^2$.</p>\n<p>(b) A full circle is $2\\pi$ radians, so 1.2 radians is the fraction $\\dfrac{1.2}{2\\pi}$ of the circle. The full circle&#x27;s area is $\\pi(80)^2 = 6400\\pi\\ \\text{cm}^2$, and $\\dfrac{1.2}{2\\pi} \\times 6400\\pi = 1.2 \\times 3200 = 3840\\ \\text{cm}^2$. ✓ Same answer.</p>\n<p>(c) The formula works because radian measure is <em>defined</em> by arc length on the circle ($s = r\\theta$), which makes the sector exactly the fraction $\\frac{\\theta}{2\\pi}$ of the whole circle — the $\\pi$&#x27;s cancel only when $\\theta$ is in radians. In degrees the fraction would be $\\frac{\\theta}{360}$, a different constant; plugging degrees into $\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$ would silently use the wrong proportion and give a wrong area.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the beam sweeps a sector of $3{,}840\\ \\text{cm}^2$.</strong></p>",
 "S4-13": "<p>(a) Complete the square for both variables:<br>$$x^2 - 6x + 9 + y^2 + 4y + 4 = 12 + 9 + 4$$<br>$$(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25$$</p>\n<p>(b) Center $(3, -2)$, radius $5$. For the dispatch team: the hospital sits at grid point $(3, -2)$, and the dispatch region extends 5 miles in every direction from it.</p>\n<p>(c) Distance from the center $(3, -2)$ to the call $(3, 3)$: $\\sqrt{(3 - 3)^2 + (3 - (-2))^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 25} = 5$ miles. The distance equals the radius exactly, so the call is <strong>on the boundary</strong> of the dispatch region — exactly 5 miles from the hospital, at the very edge of coverage (covered, but with no margin).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25$; the region is centered at the hospital $(3, -2)$ with a 5-mile radius, and the call from $(3, 3)$ lies exactly on the boundary.</strong></p>",
 "S4-14": "<p>(a) Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\\angle APB$ is an angle inscribed in a semicircle. By the theorem that an angle inscribed in a semicircle is a right angle, $\\angle APB = 90^\\circ$. (Equivalently: it intercepts the $180^\\circ$ arc $AB$, and half of $180^\\circ$ is $90^\\circ$.)</p>\n<p>(b) $\\angle ABP$ is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $AP$, which has measure $70^\\circ$. An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc:<br>$$\\angle ABP = \\frac{1}{2}(70^\\circ) = 35^\\circ$$<br>(Consistency check: the third angle is $\\angle PAB = 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 55^\\circ$, intercepting arc $PB = 110^\\circ$; and $70^\\circ + 110^\\circ = 180^\\circ$, the semicircle. ✓)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\angle APB = 90^\\circ$ (angle inscribed in a semicircle) and $\\angle ABP = 35^\\circ$ (half of its intercepted $70^\\circ$ arc).</strong></p>",
 "S4-15": "<p>(a) On the unit circle, the point at angle $t$ is $(\\cos t, \\sin t)$, and $\\sin t$ is its $y$-coordinate. Increasing the angle by $2\\pi$ is exactly one full revolution around the circle, which returns to the <em>same point</em> — so the $y$-coordinate, and hence the sine value, is unchanged: $\\sin(t + 2\\pi) = \\sin t$. That is why the monitor&#x27;s trace repeats every $2\\pi$.</p>\n<p>(b) Use periodicity to remove full revolutions: $\\dfrac{13\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{3}$, and $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$. So<br>$$\\sin\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$$</p>\n<p>(c) The angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{4}$ has its terminal side in <strong>Quadrant III</strong> (between $\\pi$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$), where the $x$-coordinate on the unit circle is negative, so $\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right) &lt; 0$. Its reference angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, giving $\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\sin$ repeats with period $2\\pi$ because a full revolution returns to the same unit-circle point; $\\sin\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, and $\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ (Quadrant III, negative).</strong></p>",
 "S4-16": "<p>(a) $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (3)^2 (8) = 72\\pi \\approx 226.19$ cubic feet.</p>\n<p>(b) Capacity $\\approx 226.19 \\times 7.48 \\approx 1691.9$, about <strong>1,692 gallons</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) $1692 \\div 120 = 14.1$, so the tank supplies <strong>14 full days</strong> of water (with about 12 gallons left over on day 15 — not a full day&#x27;s supply).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the tank holds $72\\pi \\approx 226.19\\ \\text{ft}^3 \\approx 1{,}692$ gallons, enough for 14 full days at 120 gallons per day.</strong></p>",
 "S4-17": "<p>(a) Fencing covers two widths and one length: $2w + L = 60$, so $L = 60 - 2w$. Area:<br>$$A(w) = w(60 - 2w) = -2w^2 + 60w$$<br>Domain: both $w &gt; 0$ and $L = 60 - 2w &gt; 0$ require $0 &lt; w &lt; 30$ feet.</p>\n<p>(b) $A(w) = -2w^2 + 60w$ is a downward parabola with vertex at $w = \\dfrac{-60}{2(-2)} = 15$ ft. Then $L = 60 - 2(15) = 30$ ft, and the maximum area is $A(15) = 15 \\times 30 = 450\\ \\text{ft}^2$. (Completing the square confirms it: $A(w) = -2(w - 15)^2 + 450 \\le 450$.)</p>\n<p>(c) Any reasonable unmodeled constraint, e.g.: the building&#x27;s wall may be shorter than 30 ft; an existing door or walkway fixes where the patio can go; accessibility rules require a minimum width for wheelchair turning; the ground may slope. (Any one, sensibly stated, earns the point.)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $A(w) = -2w^2 + 60w$ on $0 &lt; w &lt; 30$; the largest patio is 15 ft (perpendicular sides) by 30 ft (along the building), with maximum area $450\\ \\text{ft}^2$.</strong></p>",
 "S4-18": "<p>(a) Estimated probability $= \\dfrac{6}{200} = 0.03$, i.e., about 3%: if the two programs were equally effective, random reassignment alone would produce a difference of means at least as extreme as $-1.7$ days only about 3% of the time.</p>\n<p>(b) Since $0.03 &lt; 0.05$, the observed difference <strong>is statistically significant</strong> at the 5% level.</p>\n<p>(c) Conclusion: the clinic has statistically significant evidence that Program A genuinely reduces recovery time (by about 1.7 days on average) compared with Program B. A cause-and-effect conclusion is justified because patients were <strong>randomly assigned</strong>: random assignment balances the two groups on everything else that could affect recovery (age, severity, overall health, known and unknown), so the only systematic difference left between the groups is the program itself. In an observational comparison, patients who chose the new therapy might differ from those who didn&#x27;t, and that confounding — not the therapy — could explain the gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $p \\approx 0.03 &lt; 0.05$, so the result is significant: Program A appears to cause faster recovery, and random assignment is what licenses the causal claim.</strong></p>",
 "S4-19": "<p>(a) Study 1 is a <strong>sample survey</strong> (it collects self-reported information from a sample, with no treatment imposed). Study 2 is an <strong>experiment</strong> (a treatment — the walking program — is imposed on subjects, with a control group for comparison).</p>\n<p>(b) In Study 1, randomization means <strong>random selection</strong>: the 500 residents are chosen at random from the county, so the sample represents the whole county population and results can be generalized to all residents. In Study 2, randomization means <strong>random assignment</strong>: the 80 volunteers are randomly split between the walking program and the control group, so the two groups start out alike on average in every respect (fitness, motivation, health), isolating the program as the only systematic difference. Random selection is about <em>who gets into the study</em>; random assignment is about <em>who gets which treatment</em>.</p>\n<p>(c) Study 2 supports the causal claim. Because random assignment balanced the groups, a fitness difference at the end can be attributed to the walking program rather than to pre-existing differences. Study 1 has no treatment at all — it can describe residents&#x27; habits and associations, but people who exercise more may differ in many other ways (diet, income, health), so no causal conclusion about the program is possible from it.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Study 1 is a sample survey using random selection (for representativeness); Study 2 is an experiment using random assignment (for comparable groups) — and only Study 2 can support the claim that the program causes improved fitness.</strong></p>",
 "S4-20": "<p>(a) The total length 2.0 cm includes the cylinder plus one hemisphere radius sticking out at each end. Each hemisphere extends $0.3$ cm (one radius) beyond the cylinder, so the cylindrical section has length $2.0 - 0.3 - 0.3 = 1.4$ cm.</p>\n<p>(b) Model the capsule as a cylinder (radius 0.3 cm, height 1.4 cm) plus a sphere (radius 0.3 cm — see part (c)):<br>$$V_{\\text{cyl}} = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (0.3)^2 (1.4) = 0.126\\pi\\ \\text{cm}^3$$<br>$$V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (0.3)^3 = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (0.027) = 0.036\\pi\\ \\text{cm}^3$$<br>$$V_{\\text{total}} = 0.126\\pi + 0.036\\pi = 0.162\\pi \\approx 0.51\\ \\text{cm}^3$$</p>\n<p>(c) The pharmacist is right: the two hemispherical ends have the same radius (0.3 cm), so joined flat-face to flat-face they form exactly one complete sphere of radius 0.3 cm. That means the volume computation needs only two standard formulas — one cylinder, one sphere — instead of a cylinder plus two separate hemisphere computations (each of which would just be half the sphere&#x27;s volume anyway), so there are fewer steps and fewer places for arithmetic errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the cylindrical section is 1.4 cm long, and the capsule&#x27;s volume is $0.162\\pi \\approx 0.51\\ \\text{cm}^3$.</strong></p>",
 "S5-1": "<p>(a) Square both sides of $\\sqrt{22.5d} = 60$:<br>$$22.5d = 3600 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d = \\frac{3600}{22.5} = 160 \\text{ ft}.$$<br>Check: $\\sqrt{22.5(160)} = \\sqrt{3600} = 60$ ✓ (no extraneous solution).</p>\n<p>(b) With $d = 200$ ft, the formula gives $v = \\sqrt{22.5(200)} = \\sqrt{4500} \\approx 67.1$ mph, not 60 mph.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Her claim predicts skid marks of 160 ft; the measured 200 ft corresponds to about 67 mph, so her claim of 60 mph is not consistent with the evidence.</strong></p>",
 "S5-2": "<p>Divide by multiplying by the reciprocal:<br>$$\\frac{x^2-9}{2x} \\div \\frac{x+3}{4x^2} = \\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{2x} \\cdot \\frac{4x^2}{x+3} = (x-3) \\cdot \\frac{4x^2}{2x} = 2x(x-3) = 2x^2 - 6x.$$<br>Excluded values come from every denominator and from the divisor being zero: $2x \\ne 0$, $4x^2 \\ne 0$, and $x + 3 \\ne 0$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The fuel economy simplifies to $2x^2 - 6x$, with $x \\ne 0$ and $x \\ne -3$.</strong></p>",
 "S5-3": "<p>(a) Factor by grouping:<br>$$x^3 + 2x^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2(x + 2) + 9(x + 2) = (x + 2)(x^2 + 9).$$<br>Setting each factor to zero: $x + 2 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -2$; $x^2 + 9 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = -9 \\Rightarrow x = 3i$ or $x = -3i$.</p>\n<p>(b) The polynomial has degree 3, so the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra guarantees exactly 3 complex solutions counting multiplicity — and we found 3.</p>\n<p>(c) A physical frequency setting must be a real number, so only $x = -2$ could describe one (as a normalized/offset setting); $\\pm 3i$ are nonreal and cannot be dial settings.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The solutions are $x = -2$, $x = 3i$, and $x = -3i$ — three solutions, exactly as the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra guarantees for a cubic.</strong></p>",
 "S5-4": "<p>(a) From $d = \\dfrac{v^2}{2a}$: multiply both sides by $2a$: $2ad = v^2$; then divide by $2d$:<br>$$a = \\frac{v^2}{2d}.$$<br>(b) Substitute $v = 88$ ft/s and $d = 242$ ft:<br>$$a = \\frac{88^2}{2(242)} = \\frac{7744}{484} = 16 \\text{ ft/s}^2.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Solving for deceleration gives $a = \\dfrac{v^2}{2d}$, and the bus decelerated at 16 ft/s².</strong></p>",
 "S5-5": "<p>(a) By the Remainder Theorem, the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 3$ is $P(3)$:<br>$$P(3) = 2(27) - 7(9) + 4(3) + 3 = 54 - 63 + 12 + 3 = 6.$$<br>(b) The remainder 6 is the profit in week 3; since profit is measured in hundreds of dollars, the company made $\\$600$ in week 3.</p>\n<p>(c) Synthetic division by 3 with coefficients $2, -7, 4, 3$: bring down 2; $2\\cdot3 = 6$, $-7 + 6 = -1$; $-1\\cdot3 = -3$, $4 + (-3) = 1$; $1\\cdot3 = 3$, $3 + 3 = 6$. The remainder is 6, confirming (a).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The remainder is 6, which means the charter company&#x27;s profit in week 3 was $\\$600$.</strong></p>",
 "S5-6": "<p>(a) Average rate of change over $[0, 4]$: $\\dfrac{240 - 0}{4 - 0} = 60$ mph — the bus averaged 60 mph for the trip as a whole.</p>\n<p>(b) One-hour rates: hour 0–1: $55 - 0 = 55$ mph; hour 1–2: $130 - 55 = 75$ mph; hour 2–3: $185 - 130 = 55$ mph; hour 3–4: $240 - 185 = 55$ mph. The fastest hour was hour 1–2 at 75 mph (the open-highway stretch).</p>\n<p>(c) The 60 mph average blends the 75 mph highway hour with three 55 mph hours (towns, traffic, or hills), so it reports a typical pace that the bus actually held for only parts of the trip and conceals the one much faster stretch.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The trip average was 60 mph; the bus was fastest during the second hour (75 mph), a fact the overall average hides.</strong></p>",
 "S5-7": "<p>(a) Recursive definition: $d_1 = 60$, and $d_n = d_{n-1} + 15$ for $n \\ge 2$.</p>\n<p>(b) Deposits: week 1: $\\$60$; week 2: $\\$75$; week 3: $\\$90$; week 4: $\\$105$.</p>\n<p>(c) Weeks 5 and 6 add $\\$120$ and $\\$135$. Total: $60 + 75 + 90 + 105 + 120 + 135 = \\$585$.</p>\n<p>(d) Explicit formula: $d_n = 60 + 15(n - 1) = 15n + 45$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: With $d_1 = 60$ and $d_n = d_{n-1} + 15$, she deposits 60, 75, 90, 105 dollars in the first four weeks and saves $\\$585$ total after 6 weeks.</strong></p>",
 "S5-8": "<p>(a) Table of stations:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$t$ (years)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$P(t) = 4t^2 + 20t + 50$</td><td>50</td><td>74</td><td>106</td><td>146</td><td>194</td><td>250</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$E(t) = 40(1.6)^t$</td><td>40</td><td>64</td><td>102.4</td><td>163.84</td><td>262.14</td><td>419.43</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(b) At $t = 2$, Plan P still leads ($106 &gt; 102.4$); at $t = 3$, Plan E leads ($163.84 &gt; 146$). Plan E first moves ahead during the third year.</p>\n<p>(c) Plan E multiplies by 1.6 every year — its yearly <em>increase</em> grows as the network grows (24, then 38.4, then 61.44, … stations added per year). Plan P&#x27;s yearly increase grows only linearly (24, 32, 40, 48, …). Because the exponential&#x27;s increments keep accelerating past any polynomial&#x27;s, once E pulls ahead the gap widens every year.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Plan E first moves ahead in year 3 (about 164 vs. 146 stations), and its constant-ratio growth guarantees it stays ahead from then on.</strong></p>",
 "S5-9": "<p>From the graph&#x27;s two points, Service B&#x27;s per-mile rate is $\\dfrac{30 - 10}{10 - 0} = \\$2.00$ per mile, and its pickup fee (vertical intercept) is $\\$10$, so $C_B(x) = 2x + 10$. Service A has pickup fee $\\$4$ and rate $\\$2.50$ per mile.</p>\n<p>(a) A: \\$4 pickup, \\$2.50/mile. B: \\$10 pickup, \\$2.00/mile — A starts cheaper, B charges less per mile.</p>\n<p>(b) At 20 miles: A: $2.5(20) + 4 = \\$54$. B: $2(20) + 10 = \\$50$. Service B is cheaper by \\$4.</p>\n<p>(c) Set equal: $2.5x + 4 = 2x + 10 \\Rightarrow 0.5x = 6 \\Rightarrow x = 12$ miles.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: For a 20-mile trip, Service B costs $\\$50$ versus Service A&#x27;s $\\$54$ (B saves \\$4); the fares are equal at exactly 12 miles.</strong></p>",
 "S5-10": "<p>(a) $h(4) = 600$: after 4 minutes, the cable car is 600 ft above the valley floor.</p>\n<p>(b) $h^{-1}(1000) = 6$: the inverse swaps the roles, so this says the car reaches a height of 1000 ft after 6 minutes.</p>\n<p>(c) $h^{-1}(250) = 2$: the car is 250 ft above the floor after 2 minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $h(4) = 600$ ft at 4 minutes; $h^{-1}(1000) = 6$ minutes to reach 1000 ft; $h^{-1}(250) = 2$ minutes to reach 250 ft.</strong></p>",
 "S5-11": "<p>(a) For a point $(x, y)$ on the parabola, distance to the focus $(0, 2)$ equals distance to the directrix $y = -2$:<br>$$\\sqrt{x^2 + (y-2)^2} = |y + 2|.$$<br>Squaring: $x^2 + (y-2)^2 = (y+2)^2 \\Rightarrow x^2 + y^2 - 4y + 4 = y^2 + 4y + 4 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 8y$.</p>\n<p>(b) At $y = 8$: $x^2 = 64$, so $x = \\pm 8$. The rim runs from $-8$ cm to $8$ cm.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The reflector&#x27;s equation is $x^2 = 8y$, and at its rim ($y = 8$ cm) it is 16 cm wide.</strong></p>",
 "S5-12": "<p>(a) $150^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ radians.</p>\n<p>(b) On a unit circle, arc length equals the radian measure: $s = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\approx 2.62$ units.</p>\n<p>(c) At 10 nautical miles per unit: $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\times 10 = \\dfrac{25\\pi}{3} \\approx 26.2$ nautical miles.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $150^\\circ = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ radians; the contact travels an arc of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ units, about 26.2 nautical miles.</strong></p>",
 "S5-13": "<p>Given: In quadrilateral $ABCD$, diagonals $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ intersect at $E$ with $AE = EC$ and $BE = ED$. Prove: $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statements</th><th>Reasons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $AE = EC$ and $BE = ED$</td><td>1. Given (diagonals bisect each other)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle AEB \\cong \\angle CED$</td><td>2. Vertical angles are congruent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\triangle AEB \\cong \\triangle CED$</td><td>3. SAS congruence (steps 1 and 2)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $\\angle BAE \\cong \\angle DCE$</td><td>4. CPCTC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$</td><td>5. If alternate interior angles are congruent, the lines are parallel (angles formed by transversal $\\overline{AC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. $\\angle AEB \\cong \\angle CED$ and $\\triangle AED \\cong \\triangle CEB$</td><td>6. Vertical angles; SAS using $AE = EC$, $BE = ED$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7. $\\angle DAE \\cong \\angle BCE$, so $\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$</td><td>7. CPCTC; alternate interior angles congruent (transversal $\\overline{AC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8. $ABCD$ is a parallelogram</td><td>8. Definition of a parallelogram: both pairs of opposite sides are parallel (steps 5 and 7)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Because the bisecting diagonals force both pairs of opposite sides to be parallel, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S5-14": "<p>(a) Opposite angles of an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary. $\\angle C$ is opposite $\\angle A$: $m\\angle C = 180^\\circ - 112^\\circ = 68^\\circ$. $\\angle D$ is opposite $\\angle B$: $m\\angle D = 180^\\circ - 85^\\circ = 95^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(b) $\\angle A$ is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $BCD$, so the arc measures twice the angle: $2 \\times 112^\\circ = 224^\\circ$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $m\\angle C = 68^\\circ$ and $m\\angle D = 95^\\circ$; the intercepted arc $BCD$ measures $224^\\circ$.</strong></p>",
 "S5-15": "<p>(a) Amplitude $= 4$ cm: the piston moves 4 cm above and below the middle of its stroke. Midline $= 4$ cm: the height at the center of the stroke, halfway between bottom and top. Period $= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{20\\pi} = 0.1$ seconds: the time for one full up-and-down cycle, i.e., one crankshaft revolution.</p>\n<p>(b) $h(0.025) = 4 - 4\\cos(20\\pi \\cdot 0.025) = 4 - 4\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 4 - 4(0) = 4$ cm — the piston is exactly at mid-stroke.</p>\n<p>(c) The top occurs when $\\cos(20\\pi t) = -1$, i.e. $20\\pi t = \\pi$, so $t = 0.05$ seconds — half a period after starting at the bottom. Height: $h(0.05) = 4 + 4 = 8$ cm, the full stroke length.</p>\n<p>(d) Each cycle takes $0.1$ s, so the piston completes $\\dfrac{60}{0.1} = 600$ cycles in one minute (an engine speed of 600 rpm).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Amplitude 4 cm, midline 4 cm, period 0.1 s; after 0.025 s the piston is 4 cm up (mid-stroke); it first reaches the top — 8 cm — at 0.05 s, and completes 600 cycles per minute.</strong></p>",
 "S5-16": "<p>Problems with the report (any three, well explained, earn full credit):<br>1. <strong>Voluntary-response sample:</strong> Only people who chose to click the pop-up answered. People with strong opinions (road-trip lovers, on this site) are far more likely to respond, so the sample overstates road-trip preference.<br>2. <strong>Unrepresentative sampling frame:</strong> The poll ran on a classic-car enthusiast website. Its visitors are not a random sample of &quot;Americans&quot; — they are selected for loving cars and driving — so no result from them generalizes to the whole country.<br>3. <strong>Leading question:</strong> &quot;Don&#x27;t you agree that a great American road trip beats a cramped flight?&quot; pushes respondents toward one answer with loaded wording (&quot;great&quot; vs. &quot;cramped&quot;).<br>4. <strong>No margin of error / no random sampling:</strong> With a non-random sample, the 78% figure has no valid margin of error and cannot be extended to any population.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The data support only the narrow conclusion that 78% of the 1,200 self-selected visitors to a car-enthusiast website who answered a leading question chose &quot;road trip.&quot; They support no claim about Americans in general.</strong></p>",
 "S5-17": "<p>(a) District A density: $\\dfrac{87{,}500}{12.5} = 7{,}000$ residents/km². District B density: $\\dfrac{45{,}000}{9} = 5{,}000$ residents/km².</p>\n<p>(b) Residents per bus stop in District A: $\\dfrac{87{,}500}{25} = 3{,}500$ residents per stop.</p>\n<p>(c) District A is considerably denser (7,000 vs. 5,000 residents per km²) — more potential riders live within walking distance of each route — and each of its stops already serves about 3,500 residents.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: District A has 7,000 residents/km² (3,500 per bus stop) versus District B&#x27;s 5,000 residents/km², so District A has the stronger case for more frequent bus service.</strong></p>",
 "S5-18": "<p>(a) $V = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi (6)^2 (4) = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi (144) = 48\\pi \\approx 150.8$ ft³.</p>\n<p>(b) $150.8 \\div 27 \\approx 5.58$ yd³.</p>\n<p>(c) $5.58 \\div 3 \\approx 1.86$ loads, and partial loads still require a trip, so round up: 2 truckloads.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The pile holds $48\\pi \\approx 150.8$ ft³ of sand, about 5.58 yd³, which takes 2 truckloads to move.</strong></p>",
 "S5-19": "<p>(a) $\\hat{p} = \\dfrac{128}{250} = 0.512$, so 51.2% of the sample would ride the express at least weekly.</p>\n<p>(b) Margin of error $\\approx 2 \\times 0.032 = 0.064$. Plausible interval: $0.512 \\pm 0.064$, i.e. about $0.448$ to $0.576$ (44.8% to 57.6%).</p>\n<p>(c) No — not conclusively. The point estimate (51.2%) is above 45%, but the interval includes values below the threshold, down to about 44.8%. The true proportion could fall short of 45%, so the agency cannot yet conclude the threshold is met.</p>\n<p>(d) Quadrupling the sample size roughly halves the sampling variability: the simulation standard deviation would drop to about $0.016$, giving a margin of error of about $0.032$ and an interval of roughly $0.480$ to $0.544$ (48.0% to 54.4%). That interval lies entirely above 45%, so with the same sample proportion from 1,000 riders, the agency could conclude the threshold is met.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\hat{p} = 0.512$; margin of error $\\approx 0.064$; plausible interval about 44.8% to 57.6% — the 45% threshold is not yet confirmed; a sample of 1,000 would roughly halve the margin of error and clear the threshold.</strong></p>",
 "S5-20": "<p>(a) A vertical plane across the tank cuts the circular end profile straight on, producing a <strong>circle</strong> of radius 4 ft.</p>\n<p>(b) A horizontal plane through the central axis slices the cylinder lengthwise, producing a <strong>rectangle</strong> whose length is the tank&#x27;s length, 20 ft, and whose width is the diameter, $2 \\times 4 = 8$ ft: a 20 ft by 8 ft rectangle.</p>\n<p>(c) A slanted plane meets the curved wall at an angle, stretching the circular profile in one direction, producing an <strong>ellipse</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (a) circle; (b) rectangle, 20 ft by 8 ft; (c) ellipse.</strong></p>",
 "S6-1": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, $1 - 2i$:<br>$$\\frac{7 + 4i}{1 + 2i} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 2i}{1 - 2i} = \\frac{(7 + 4i)(1 - 2i)}{(1 + 2i)(1 - 2i)}.$$<br>Numerator: $7 - 14i + 4i - 8i^2 = 7 - 10i + 8 = 15 - 10i$ (using $i^2 = -1$).<br>Denominator: $1 - (2i)^2 = 1 + 4 = 5$.<br>$$\\frac{15 - 10i}{5} = 3 - 2i.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The current is $I = 3 - 2i$; the conjugate turns the denominator into the real number 5, making the quotient a standard complex number.</strong></p>",
 "S6-2": "<p>The common denominator is $(x - 1)(x + 4)$:<br>$$\\frac{4}{x-1} + \\frac{2}{x+4} = \\frac{4(x+4) + 2(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+4)} = \\frac{4x + 16 + 2x - 2}{(x-1)(x+4)} = \\frac{6x + 14}{(x-1)(x+4)} = \\frac{2(3x + 7)}{(x-1)(x+4)}.$$<br>Excluded values: $x \\ne 1$ and $x \\ne -4$ (from the original denominators).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The combined drainage rate is $\\dfrac{2(3x + 7)}{(x-1)(x+4)}$ gallons per minute, with $x \\ne 1$ and $x \\ne -4$.</strong></p>",
 "S6-3": "<p>Note $x^2 - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3)$. Multiply every term by the LCD $(x-3)(x+3)$:<br>$$x(x + 3) - 12(x - 3) = 18$$<br>$$x^2 + 3x - 12x + 36 = 18 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x^2 - 9x + 18 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (x - 3)(x - 6) = 0.$$<br>Candidates: $x = 3$ and $x = 6$.<br>• Check $x = 3$: the original equation has denominators $x - 3 = 0$ and $x^2 - 9 = 0$ — undefined. $x = 3$ is <strong>extraneous</strong>, introduced when we multiplied by an expression that can be zero.<br>• Check $x = 6$: $\\dfrac{6}{3} - \\dfrac{12}{9} = 2 - \\dfrac{4}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$; RHS: $\\dfrac{18}{36 - 9} = \\dfrac{18}{27} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ ✓.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The only valid solution is $x = 6$ ($x = 3$ is extraneous), so the formula calls for a crew of 6 members.</strong></p>",
 "S6-4": "<p>(a) The term $\\pi r^2$ is the area of the circular base (one flat disk of sheet metal). The term $2\\pi r h$ is the lateral surface area: $2\\pi r$ is the circumference of the base circle, and multiplying by $h$ &quot;unrolls&quot; the curved side into a rectangle of width $2\\pi r$ and height $h$. There is no second $\\pi r^2$ term because the planter is open at the top.</p>\n<p>(b) With $r = 1.5$ ft and $h = 2$ ft:<br>$$A = \\pi (1.5)^2 + 2\\pi (1.5)(2) = 2.25\\pi + 6\\pi = 8.25\\pi \\approx 25.9 \\text{ ft}^2.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The base needs $\\pi r^2$ and the side needs $2\\pi r h$; for $r = 1.5$ ft, $h = 2$ ft, the planter requires $8.25\\pi \\approx 25.9$ ft² of sheet metal.</strong></p>",
 "S6-5": "<p>(a) Factor out $x$, then factor the quadratic:<br>$$P(x) = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) = x(x - 4)(x + 2).$$<br>Zeros: $x = 0$, $x = 4$, and $x = -2$.</p>\n<p>(b) $P$ is a cubic with positive leading coefficient, so as $x \\to -\\infty$, $P(x) \\to -\\infty$, and as $x \\to \\infty$, $P(x) \\to \\infty$.</p>\n<p>(c) In context, only $x \\ge 0$ makes sense (months since launch). $P(2) = 8 - 8 - 16 = -16 &lt; 0$: between launch ($x = 0$) and month 4, profit is negative — the shop is losing money. At $x = 4$ profit is zero (break-even), and for $x &gt; 4$ profit is positive and growing. The zero at $x = -2$ lies outside the context&#x27;s domain (two months before launch).</p>\n<p>(d) Sketch: the graph crosses the horizontal axis at $-2$, $0$, and $4$; it rises from the lower left, dips below the axis between 0 and 4 (reaching a local minimum in that interval), crosses at 4, and rises steeply to the upper right. The vertical intercept is $P(0) = 0$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $P(x) = x(x-4)(x+2)$ with zeros $-2, 0, 4$; the shop loses money during its first 4 months and breaks even for good at month 4.</strong></p>",
 "S6-6": "<p>(a) Area is length times width; the patio is square, so both factors are $(x + 6)$:<br>$$A(x) = (x + 6)(x + 6) = x^2 + 12x + 36 \\text{ ft}^2.$$<br>(b) Cost at \\$9 per square foot: $C(x) = 9A(x) = 9x^2 + 108x + 324$ dollars. At $x = 10$: $A(10) = 100 + 120 + 36 = 256$ ft², so $C(10) = 9 \\times 256 = \\$2{,}304$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $A(x) = x^2 + 12x + 36$ ft² and $C(x) = 9x^2 + 108x + 324$; for an original 10 ft patio, the expanded patio costs $\\$2,304$ in pavers.</strong></p>",
 "S6-7": "<p>(a) The graph is: a horizontal segment at $C = 150$ from $h = 0$ (open circle at $h = 0$, since a 0-hour job isn&#x27;t charged) to $h = 2$ (closed circle at $(2, 150)$); then a line with slope 45 from the closed point $(2, 150)$ up to $(6, 330)$ (closed); then a steeper line with slope 55 starting at $(6, 330)$ and rising to $(9, 495)$. The pieces meet at $h = 2$ and $h = 6$ (check: $150 + 45(6 - 2) = 330$ ✓), so the graph is continuous — no jumps.</p>\n<p>(b) 5-hour job (second piece): $C(5) = 150 + 45(5 - 2) = 150 + 135 = \\$285$. 7-hour job (third piece): $C(7) = 330 + 55(7 - 6) = \\$385$.</p>\n<p>(c) The third piece is steeper because the hourly rate rises from \\$45/hr to \\$55/hr after 6 hours — an overtime premium for long jobs.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: A 5-hour job costs $\\$285$ and a 7-hour job costs $\\$385$; the graph is continuous with slopes 0, 45, and 55 reflecting the flat fee, standard rate, and overtime rate.</strong></p>",
 "S6-8": "<p>First composition — apply $g$, then $C$:<br>$$C(g(C)) = 135\\left(\\frac{C - 75}{135}\\right) + 75 = (C - 75) + 75 = C. \\checkmark$$<br>Second composition — apply $C$, then $g$:<br>$$g(C(x)) = \\frac{(135x + 75) - 75}{135} = \\frac{135x}{135} = x. \\checkmark$$<br>Because each composition returns its input unchanged, each function exactly undoes the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Both compositions simplify to the identity ($C(g(C)) = C$ and $g(C(x)) = x$), which verifies that $C$ and $g$ are inverse functions — the office&#x27;s formula correctly recovers yardage from a total charge.</strong></p>",
 "S6-9": "<p>(a) Set $320{,}000(1.045)^t = 500{,}000$ and divide: $(1.045)^t = \\dfrac{500{,}000}{320{,}000} = 1.5625$. Writing the exponent as a logarithm:<br>$$t = \\frac{\\log(1.5625)}{\\log(1.045)} \\quad \\left(\\text{equivalently } t = \\log_{1.045}(1.5625)\\right).$$<br>(b) Evaluating: $\\log(1.5625) \\approx 0.19382$ and $\\log(1.045) \\approx 0.019116$, so $t \\approx \\dfrac{0.19382}{0.019116} \\approx 10.1$ years. (Check: $320{,}000(1.045)^{10.1} \\approx \\$499{,}000$, close to \\$500,000 ✓.)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $t = \\dfrac{\\log(1.5625)}{\\log(1.045)} \\approx 10.1$ years — the renovated home reaches a value of $\\$500,000$ after about 10 years.</strong></p>",
 "S6-10": "<p>(a) Domain: $0 \\le t \\le 9$ (the lift lasts 9 minutes). Range: $0 \\le h \\le 24$ (the beam goes from ground level up to 24 ft).</p>\n<p>(b) On the rising segment, height grows at $\\dfrac{24}{4} = 6$ ft/min, so $h(2) = 12$: two minutes in, the beam is 12 ft above the ground.</p>\n<p>(c) The beam is at its maximum height of 24 ft for the whole plateau, $4 \\le t \\le 6$ minutes.</p>\n<p>(d) On the descent, the beam drops 24 ft in 3 minutes, a rate of 8 ft/min. At $t = 7.5$ (1.5 minutes into the descent): $h(7.5) = 24 - 8(1.5) = 12$: the beam is 12 ft up, halfway down.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Domain $0 \\le t \\le 9$ min, range $0 \\le h \\le 24$ ft; $h(2) = 12$ ft (rising), the beam tops out at 24 ft from minute 4 to minute 6, and $h(7.5) = 12$ ft (lowering).</strong></p>",
 "S6-11": "<p>Given triangle $ABC$, line $\\ell$ through $A$ is parallel to $\\overline{BC}$, with $D$–$A$–$E$ collinear on $\\ell$ in that order. Prove $m\\angle BAC + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statements</th><th>Reasons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $\\ell \\parallel \\overline{BC}$ through $A$, with $D$–$A$–$E$ on $\\ell$</td><td>1. Given (construction of the figure)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle DAB \\cong \\angle B$ (i.e., $\\angle DAB$ and $\\angle ABC$ have equal measure)</td><td>2. Alternate interior angles are congruent when lines are parallel (transversal $\\overline{AB}$ cutting $\\ell$ and $\\overline{BC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\angle EAC \\cong \\angle C$ (i.e., $\\angle EAC$ and $\\angle ACB$ have equal measure)</td><td>3. Alternate interior angles are congruent when lines are parallel (transversal $\\overline{AC}$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $\\angle DAE$ is a straight angle, so $m\\angle DAB + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle EAC = 180^\\circ$</td><td>4. Angles on a straight line sum to $180^\\circ$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $m\\angle B + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$</td><td>5. Substitution of steps 2 and 3 into step 4</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The two outer angles at $A$ on the parallel line equal $\\angle B$ and $\\angle C$, and together with $\\angle BAC$ they form a straight angle — so the interior angles of the triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S6-12": "<p>(a) A central angle has the same measure as its intercepted arc: arc $AB = 84^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(b) $\\angle ACB$ has its vertex on the circle and intercepts arc $AB$ (the minor arc, $84^\\circ$), so $m\\angle ACB = \\tfrac{1}{2}(84^\\circ) = 42^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(c) $\\angle ADB$ intercepts the <strong>major</strong> arc $AB$, which measures $360^\\circ - 84^\\circ = 276^\\circ$, so $m\\angle ADB = \\tfrac{1}{2}(276^\\circ) = 138^\\circ$. (Note $42^\\circ + 138^\\circ = 180^\\circ$: $ACBD$ is an inscribed quadrilateral, whose opposite angles are supplementary — a useful check.)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Arc $AB$ measures $84^\\circ$; the inscribed angle from the major arc is $42^\\circ$, and from the minor arc is $138^\\circ$ because it intercepts the $276^\\circ$ major arc.</strong></p>",
 "S6-13": "<p>(a) Group and complete the square in each variable:<br>$$x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 4y = 12$$<br>$$(x^2 - 6x + 9) + (y^2 + 4y + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4$$<br>$$(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25.$$<br>(b) Center: $(3, -2)$; radius: $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ ft.</p>\n<p>(c) The table goes at the center, $(3, -2)$ on the survey grid.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: In standard form the patio is $(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25$, a circle with center $(3, -2)$ and radius 5 ft, so the table sits at $(3, -2)$.</strong></p>",
 "S6-14": "<p>(a) $405^\\circ = 360^\\circ + 45^\\circ$. By periodicity (period $360^\\circ$), $\\sin 405^\\circ = \\sin 45^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\cos 405^\\circ = \\cos 45^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The terminal side is in Quadrant I (after one full turn), where both values are positive ✓.</p>\n<p>(b) $-210^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-210^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 150^\\circ$, whose reference angle is $30^\\circ$ and whose terminal side lies in Quadrant II. So $\\sin(-210^\\circ) = \\sin 150^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ (sine is positive in QII ✓) and $\\cos(-210^\\circ) = \\cos 150^\\circ = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ (cosine is negative in QII ✓). Equivalently, sine is odd and cosine is even: $\\sin(-210^\\circ) = -\\sin 210^\\circ = -(-\\tfrac12) = \\tfrac12$ and $\\cos(-210^\\circ) = \\cos 210^\\circ = -\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.</p>\n<p>(c) $405^\\circ$: Quadrant I, both signs positive. $-210^\\circ$: Quadrant II (coterminal with $150^\\circ$), sine positive, cosine negative — matching the computed values.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\sin 405^\\circ = \\cos 405^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$; $\\sin(-210^\\circ) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\cos(-210^\\circ) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, with terminal sides in Quadrants I and II respectively.</strong></p>",
 "S6-15": "<p>(a) Semicircle: $\\theta = \\pi$. For $r = 2$: $s = r\\theta = 2\\pi \\approx 6.28$ ft. For $r = 3$: $s = 3\\pi \\approx 9.42$ ft.</p>\n<p>(b) Ratio of arc lengths: $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{3}{2}$, which equals the ratio of the radii, $\\dfrac{3}{2}$. ✓</p>\n<p>(c) Derivation: The intercepted arc is the fraction $\\dfrac{\\theta}{2\\pi}$ of the full circumference $2\\pi r$:<br>$$s = \\frac{\\theta}{2\\pi} \\cdot 2\\pi r = r\\theta.$$<br>For a fixed angle $\\theta$, $s$ is $r$ multiplied by the constant $\\theta$, so arc length is directly proportional to the radius (equivalently, all circles are similar, so scaling the radius scales every arc by the same factor).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The edging lengths are $2\\pi \\approx 6.28$ ft and $3\\pi \\approx 9.42$ ft, in the same $3:2$ ratio as the radii, because $s = r\\theta$ makes arc length proportional to radius for a fixed angle.</strong></p>",
 "S6-16": "<p>(a) With $h = \\dfrac{32}{x^2}$ (from $V = x^2 h = 32$), material is $A(x) = x^2 + \\dfrac{128}{x}$:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$x$ (ft)</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>4.5</th><th>5</th><th>6</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$A(x)$ (ft²)</td><td>68</td><td>51.67</td><td>48</td><td>48.69</td><td>50.6</td><td>57.33</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The values decrease until $x = 4$ and increase afterward, so the minimum is at (or very near) $x = 4$ ft.</p>\n<p>(b) Height: $h = \\dfrac{32}{4^2} = 2$ ft. Minimum material: $A(4) = 16 + 32 = 48$ ft².</p>\n<p>(c) With a very small base, the bin must be very tall to hold 32 ft³, so the four side walls use a lot of material. With a very large base, the base panel itself is huge. The optimum balances the two: base area and total side area trade off, meeting near $x = 4$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: A square base of 4 ft by 4 ft with height 2 ft minimizes the material at 48 ft².</strong></p>",
 "S6-17": "<p>(a) Base area $B = 12 \\times 12 = 144$ ft². Volume:<br>$$V = \\frac{1}{3}Bh = \\frac{1}{3}(144)(5) = 240 \\text{ ft}^3.$$<br>(b) Convert: $240 \\div 27 \\approx 8.9$ yd³.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The pyramid attic holds 240 ft³, so the contractor needs about 8.9 cubic yards of spray-foam insulation.</strong></p>",
 "S6-18": "<p>(a) Design 1 is a <strong>sample survey</strong> (it asks homeowners questions; no treatment is imposed and responses are self-reported). Design 2 is an <strong>observational study</strong> (researchers record what already happened — owners chose their own primers — without assigning anything). Design 3 is an <strong>experiment</strong> (a treatment — primer type — is imposed by the researchers).</p>\n<p>(b) Only Design 3 can support a causal claim. In Designs 1 and 2, primer choice is tangled with confounding variables: owners who buy premium primer may also prepare surfaces better, hire professionals, or live in drier areas, any of which could explain less peeling. Design 3 breaks that link by assigning the primer itself.</p>\n<p>(c) Random assignment makes the two groups alike on average in every other respect (home age, sun exposure, painter skill, prep work), so a difference in peeling can be attributed to the primer. The conclusion applies to the population the participants represent — homes like the 60 recruited homes in that town — not automatically to all homes everywhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Survey, observational study, and experiment respectively; only the randomized experiment supports the claim that the primer causes less peeling, and its conclusion extends to homes similar to those in the study.</strong></p>",
 "S6-19": "<p>(a) Proportion of simulations with a difference of $\\$22$ or more: $\\dfrac{12}{500} = 0.024$, or 2.4%.</p>\n<p>(b) $2.4\\% &lt; 5\\%$, so yes — the observed difference is statistically significant. If insulation type truly made no difference, a gap this large would occur by chance in only about 2.4% of random assignments.</p>\n<p>(c) Because the homes were randomly assigned, the builder can conclude that the new insulation package is the likely cause of the greater savings — the new-insulation homes saved on average about $\\$22$ more per month, and chance alone is an implausible explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Only 2.4% of simulations matched or beat the observed $\\$22$ difference, so the result is significant at the 5% level; the evidence supports a real savings advantage for the new insulation.</strong></p>",
 "S6-20": "<p>(a) Rectangle: $30 \\times 10 = 300$ ft². Gable (triangle): $\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 30 \\times 6 = 90$ ft². Gross wall area: $300 + 90 = 390$ ft². Cutouts: two windows $2 \\times (3 \\times 4) = 24$ ft²; door $3 \\times 7 = 21$ ft²; total cutouts $45$ ft². Siding area: $390 - 45 = 345$ ft².</p>\n<p>(b) $345 \\div 100 = 3.45$ squares, and partial squares can&#x27;t be bought, so round up: 4 squares.</p>\n<p>(c) Cost: $4 \\times \\$185 = \\$740$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The wall needs 345 ft² of siding; the homeowner must buy 4 squares, costing $\\$740$.</strong></p>",
 "S7-1": "<p>(a) Divide $2x^2 + 7x + 3$ by $x + 2$:<br>• $2x^2 \\div x = 2x$; multiply: $2x(x + 2) = 2x^2 + 4x$; subtract: $3x + 3$.<br>• $3x \\div x = 3$; multiply: $3(x + 2) = 3x + 6$; subtract: $-3$.</p>\n<p>$$T(x) = 2x + 3 - \\frac{3}{x + 2}$$</p>\n<p>(b) As $x \\to \\infty$, the term $\\dfrac{3}{x + 2} \\to 0$, so $T(x)$ is governed by the quotient $2x + 3$: average processing time grows roughly linearly, increasing by about $2$ ms per additional cluster in the long run.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $T(x) = 2x + 3 - \\dfrac{3}{x + 2}$; for large $x$ the processing time behaves like $2x + 3$ ms because the remainder term shrinks to zero.</strong></p>",
 "S7-2": "<p>(a) Factor by grouping:<br>$$x^3 - 3x^2 + 9x - 27 = x^2(x - 3) + 9(x - 3) = (x - 3)(x^2 + 9)$$<br>• $x - 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 3$.<br>• $x^2 + 9 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = -9 \\Rightarrow x = 3i$ or $x = -3i$.</p>\n<p>(b) The polynomial has degree $3$, so by the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra it has exactly $3$ complex solutions counting multiplicity — and we found exactly $3$: $3$, $3i$, $-3i$.</p>\n<p>(c) Only $x = 3$ is a real number, so only it can describe a physical responsiveness setting. The solutions $3i$ and $-3i$ are nonreal and must be rejected in context, though they are required for a complete algebraic solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $x = 3$, $x = 3i$, $x = -3i$ — three solutions, matching the degree; only $x = 3$ is usable for the drone.</strong></p>",
 "S7-3": "<p>(a) Service A: $C = 12 + 0.80h$. Service B: $C = 4 + 1.60h$. Graphing both lines, A&#x27;s line starts higher (intercept $12$) but rises more slowly; B&#x27;s starts at $4$ and rises faster, so the lines cross once.</p>\n<p>(b) Set the expressions equal: $12 + 0.80h = 4 + 1.60h \\Rightarrow 8 = 0.80h \\Rightarrow h = 10$. At $h = 10$, $C = 12 + 0.80(10) = \\$20$. The intersection point $(10, 20)$ lies on both graphs, so its $h$-coordinate makes the two cost expressions equal — that is exactly the solution of $f(h) = g(h)$.</p>\n<p>(c) At $6$ hours: A costs $12 + 4.80 = \\$16.80$; B costs $4 + 9.60 = \\$13.60$ → <strong>B is cheaper</strong>. At $15$ hours: A costs $12 + 12 = \\$24$; B costs $4 + 24 = \\$28$ → <strong>A is cheaper</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The plans break even at $10$ hours per month ($\\$20$ either way); below $10$ hours choose Service B, above $10$ hours choose Service A.</strong></p>",
 "S7-4": "<p>(a) Common denominator $(t + 2)(t + 5)$:<br>$$\\frac{t}{t + 2} - \\frac{3}{t + 5} = \\frac{t(t + 5) - 3(t + 2)}{(t + 2)(t + 5)} = \\frac{t^2 + 5t - 3t - 6}{(t + 2)(t + 5)} = \\frac{t^2 + 2t - 6}{(t + 2)(t + 5)}$$</p>\n<p>(b) The denominator is zero when $t = -2$ or $t = -5$, so both values are excluded from the domain of the combined expression — at those values the original fractions are undefined, regardless of the time interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Server 1 is ahead by $\\dfrac{t^2 + 2t - 6}{(t + 2)(t + 5)}$ of the file after $t$ minutes, with $t \\neq -2, -5$.</strong></p>",
 "S7-5": "<p>(a) Rearrange $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$:<br>$$\\frac{T}{2\\pi} = \\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left(\\frac{T}{2\\pi}\\right)^2 = \\frac{L}{g} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; L = \\frac{gT^2}{4\\pi^2}$$</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute $g = 9.8$ and $T = 1.2$:<br>$$L = \\frac{9.8(1.2)^2}{4\\pi^2} = \\frac{9.8(1.44)}{39.478} = \\frac{14.112}{39.478} \\approx 0.36\\ \\text{m}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $L = \\dfrac{gT^2}{4\\pi^2}$, and a $1.2$-second period requires a swing arm of about $0.36$ m (about $36$ cm).</strong></p>",
 "S7-6": "<p>(a)–(b) $f(x) = \\log_2(x + 4) - 1$:<br>• Domain: $x + 4 &gt; 0 \\Rightarrow x &gt; -4$; vertical asymptote $x = -4$.<br>• $x$-intercept: $\\log_2(x + 4) = 1 \\Rightarrow x + 4 = 2 \\Rightarrow x = -2$, giving $(-2, 0)$.<br>• $y$-intercept: $f(0) = \\log_2 4 - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1$, giving $(0, 1)$.<br>• Range: all real numbers. The curve passes through $(-3, -1)$, $(-2, 0)$, $(0, 1)$, $(4, 2)$ and rises slowly to the right.</p>\n<p>(c) The graph is the graph of $y = \\log_2 x$ shifted $4$ units left and $1$ unit down.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Asymptote $x = -4$; intercepts $(-2, 0)$ and $(0, 1)$; domain $x &gt; -4$; range all real numbers; the parent log graph shifted left $4$ and down $1$.</strong></p>",
 "S7-7": "<p>(a) $g(x) = f(2x) = (2x)^3 - 2x = 8x^3 - 2x$. Replacing $x$ by $2x$ compresses the graph horizontally by a factor of $\\tfrac{1}{2}$: every feature of the waveform happens in half the time, which matches the tone playing at double speed (the wave&#x27;s width is halved, so its frequency doubles).</p>\n<p>(b) $f(-x) = (-x)^3 - (-x) = -x^3 + x = -(x^3 - x) = -f(x)$. Since $f(-x) = -f(x)$, $f$ is <strong>odd</strong>, and its graph has symmetry about the origin.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $g(x) = 8x^3 - 2x$ is a horizontal compression of $f$ by a factor of $\\tfrac12$; $f$ is odd, so the waveform is symmetric about the origin.</strong></p>",
 "S7-8": "<p>(a)</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$t$ (days)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$L(t) = 500 + 120t$</td><td>500</td><td>620</td><td>740</td><td>860</td><td>980</td><td>1100</td><td>1220</td><td>1340</td><td>1460</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$E(t) = 300(1.25)^t$</td><td>300</td><td>375</td><td>468.75</td><td>585.94</td><td>732.42</td><td>915.53</td><td>1144.41</td><td>1430.51</td><td>1788.14</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(b) At $t = 6$, $L = 1220 &gt; E \\approx 1144$. At $t = 7$, $E \\approx 1430.51 &gt; L = 1340$. So Title E first passes Title L on <strong>day 7</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) Title L adds a constant $120$ hundred players per day, while Title E multiplies by $1.25$ each day, so E&#x27;s daily gains keep growing ($75$, then $93.75$, then $117.19$, …). Once a growing gain overtakes a constant gain, the exponential pulls away permanently — by day 8 the gap is already about $328$ hundred players and widening.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Title E first passes Title L on day 7 (about $143{,}051$ vs. $134{,}000$ players), and stays ahead because exponential growth by a constant ratio eventually exceeds linear growth by a constant amount.</strong></p>",
 "S7-9": "<p>(a) Start with $P = 4m + 100$. Subtract the bonus, then divide by the rate:<br>$$P - 100 = 4m \\;\\Rightarrow\\; m = \\frac{P - 100}{4}$$</p>\n<p>(b) At $P = 900$: $m = \\dfrac{900 - 100}{4} = \\dfrac{800}{4} = 200$ minutes.</p>\n<p>(c) Part (b) gives points and asks for minutes — the reverse direction of the original function, which takes minutes to points. The inverse is built exactly for that reversal.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The inverse is $m = \\dfrac{P - 100}{4}$; a viewer with $900$ points has watched $200$ minutes (3 hours 20 minutes).</strong></p>",
 "S7-10": "<p>(a) The charge starts at $100\\%$ when $t = 0$ and reaches $0\\%$ when $100 - 4t = 0$, i.e., $t = 25$. Practical domain: $0 \\le t \\le 25$ hours. Corresponding range: $0 \\le C \\le 100$ percent.</p>\n<p>(b) Negative times would mean &quot;before takeoff,&quot; and charge above $100\\%$ is impossible; times beyond $25$ hours would give a negative charge, but a battery cannot go below empty — the drone has already landed/been forced down. The linear model only describes reality between those endpoints.</p>\n<p>(c) The drone can stay airborne until the charge reaches $0\\%$: $25$ hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Domain $0 \\le t \\le 25$ h, range $0 \\le C \\le 100\\%$; maximum flight time is $25$ hours.</strong></p>",
 "S7-11": "<p>(a) For $p(t) = 2.5\\sin(20\\pi t)$:<br>• Amplitude $= 2.5$ mm — the cone moves at most $2.5$ mm from its rest position.<br>• Midline $= 0$ mm — the cone oscillates evenly around its rest position.<br>• Period $= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{20\\pi} = 0.1$ s — one full back-and-forth cycle takes a tenth of a second.<br>• Frequency $= \\dfrac{1}{0.1} = 10$ Hz — the cone completes $10$ cycles per second.</p>\n<p>(b) Peak-to-peak distance $= 2 \\times 2.5 = 5$ mm.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Amplitude $2.5$ mm, midline $0$, period $0.1$ s, frequency $10$ cycles per second; the cone travels $5$ mm from one extreme to the other.</strong></p>",
 "S7-12": "<p>(a) Complete the square:<br>$$x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 4y = 3 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 2)^2 - 4 = 3 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 16$$<br>Center $(3, -2)$ — the router&#x27;s location — and radius $4$ m.</p>\n<p>(b) Distance from $(3, -2)$ to the desk at $(6, 2)$:<br>$$d = \\sqrt{(6 - 3)^2 + (2 - (-2))^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\\ \\text{m}$$<br>Since $5 &gt; 4$, the desk is outside the coverage circle.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The router is at $(3, -2)$ with a $4$ m coverage radius; the desk at $(6, 2)$ is $5$ m away, so it is not covered.</strong></p>",
 "S7-13": "<p>(a) The central angle $\\angle AOB$ and the inscribed angle $\\angle ACB$ intercept the same arc $AB$. The arc&#x27;s measure equals the central angle, $96^\\circ$. The inscribed angle is half its intercepted arc: $\\angle ACB = \\tfrac{1}{2}(96^\\circ) = 48^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(b) The central angle&#x27;s sides pass through the center, so its measure is defined to equal the measure of arc $AB$. An inscribed angle with vertex on the circle intercepts that same arc, and the inscribed angle theorem states its measure is half the arc&#x27;s measure — hence exactly half the central angle on the same arc.</p>\n<p>(c) No. Any point $C&#x27;$ on the same arc (the major arc, opposite the intercepted arc) forms an inscribed angle intercepting the same arc $AB$, so $\\angle AC&#x27;B$ is also $48^\\circ$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\angle ACB = 48^\\circ$, half the $96^\\circ$ central angle because both intercept arc $AB$; the angle stays $48^\\circ$ anywhere on the same arc.</strong></p>",
 "S7-14": "<p>(a) $150^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ radians.</p>\n<p>(b) $s = r\\theta = 45 \\times \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} = 37.5\\pi \\approx 117.8$ cm.</p>\n<p>(c) Radian measure is defined as the ratio of arc length to radius, $\\theta = s/r$, on a circle. Because of that definition, $s = r\\theta$ holds exactly when $\\theta$ is in radians; degree measure is an arbitrary division of a turn into $360$ parts and would require an extra conversion factor.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $150^\\circ = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ radians, and the blade tip travels $37.5\\pi \\approx 117.8$ cm.</strong></p>",
 "S7-15": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> $\\triangle ABC$; line $\\ell$ through $A$ parallel to $\\overleftrightarrow{BC}$, with $D$–$A$–$E$ on $\\ell$.<br><strong>Prove:</strong> $m\\angle BAC + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statements</th><th>Reasons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $\\ell \\parallel \\overleftrightarrow{BC}$</td><td>1. Given (construction through $A$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle DAB \\cong \\angle B$</td><td>2. Alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines cut by transversal $AB$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\angle EAC \\cong \\angle C$</td><td>3. Alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines cut by transversal $AC$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $m\\angle DAB + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle EAC = 180^\\circ$</td><td>4. $D$–$A$–$E$ is a straight line, so the angles at $A$ on one side form a straight angle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $m\\angle B + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$</td><td>5. Substitution using steps 2 and 3</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Conclusion: The interior angles of $\\triangle ABC$ sum to $180^\\circ$, for any triangle. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S7-16": "<p>(a) Rotating a right triangle about one of its legs sweeps out a <strong>cone</strong>. The leg on the axis (the vertical leg, $4$ cm) becomes the height, and the horizontal leg ($3$ cm) sweeps out the circular base, so the cone has radius $3$ cm and height $4$ cm.</p>\n<p>(b) The axis of rotation becomes the cone&#x27;s central axis/height; the leg perpendicular to the axis becomes the radius because every point of it traces a circle; the hypotenuse sweeps the slanted lateral surface.</p>\n<p>(c) Rotating the rectangle about its $5$-cm side produces a <strong>cylinder</strong> with height $5$ cm (the side on the axis) and radius $2$ cm (the side perpendicular to the axis sweeping the circular bases).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The triangle generates a cone of radius $3$ cm and height $4$ cm; the rectangle generates a cylinder of radius $2$ cm and height $5$ cm.</strong></p>",
 "S7-17": "<p>(a) $\\hat{p} = \\dfrac{74}{200} = 0.37$, i.e., $37\\%$ of the sample would join the club.</p>\n<p>(b) The simulation&#x27;s middle $95\\%$ range gives the plausible interval directly: about $0.305$ to $0.435$, i.e., <strong>$30.5\\%$ to $43.5\\%$</strong> of all $1{,}800$ students.</p>\n<p>(c) Yes. Even the lowest plausible value, $30.5\\%$, exceeds the $30\\%$ funding threshold, so funding is justified — though with little room to spare.</p>\n<p>(d) A single sample proportion varies from sample to sample just by chance. The simulation shows how much that variation is, so the funding decision rests on the whole plausible range of values rather than on one possibly lucky or unlucky survey.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\hat{p} = 0.37$; plausible interval $\\approx 30.5\\%$–$43.5\\%$ from the simulation. Funding is justified because the entire interval lies above the $30\\%$ threshold.</strong></p>",
 "S7-18": "<p>(a) $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (2)^2 (0.5) = 2\\pi \\approx 6.28\\ \\text{cm}^3$.</p>\n<p>(b) Mass $=$ density $\\times$ volume $= 8.5 \\times 2\\pi \\approx 8.5 \\times 6.283 = 53.4$ g per token.</p>\n<p>(c) $200 \\times 53.4\\ \\text{g} = 10{,}681\\ \\text{g} \\approx 10.7$ kg. Since $10.7 &lt; 12$, the tokens fit under the limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: One token has volume $2\\pi \\approx 6.28\\ \\text{cm}^3$ and mass about $53.4$ g; $200$ tokens weigh about $10.7$ kg, so the box can ship under the $12$ kg contents limit.</strong></p>",
 "S7-19": "<p>(a) This is an <strong>observational study</strong>: the researchers only recorded a characteristic the gamers already had (whether they regularly drank energy drinks). No treatment was imposed or assigned by the researchers.</p>\n<p>(b) The causal conclusion is not justified because: (1) <strong>self-selection/confounding</strong> — gamers who choose energy drinks may differ from the others in sleep habits, experience, practice time, or competitiveness, any of which could explain faster reaction times; (2) <strong>no random assignment</strong>, so the groups were not balanced on other variables, and the direction of influence is unclear (e.g., serious gamers may both train more and drink more energy drinks).</p>\n<p>(c) Run an experiment: randomly assign the $60$ gamers to an energy-drink group or a comparable control (e.g., a similar-tasting non-caffeinated drink), keep conditions otherwise the same, and compare reaction times. Random assignment balances confounders, so a difference could be attributed to the drink.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Observational study — association only. Self-selection and confounding block the causal claim; random assignment to drink vs. control is the fix.</strong></p>",
 "S7-20": "<p>(a) $V = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (25)^3 = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (15{,}625) = \\dfrac{62{,}500\\pi}{3} \\approx 65{,}449.8\\ \\text{cm}^3$.</p>\n<p>(b) $65{,}449.8 \\div 1{,}000 \\approx 65.4$ L.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The tank&#x27;s volume is $\\dfrac{62{,}500\\pi}{3} \\approx 65{,}449.8\\ \\text{cm}^3$, so it holds about $65.4$ liters of water.</strong></p>",
 "S8-1": "<p>(a) Square both sides of $\\sqrt{x + 7} = x - 5$:<br>$$x + 7 = (x - 5)^2 = x^2 - 10x + 25 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x^2 - 11x + 18 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (x - 2)(x - 9) = 0$$<br>Candidates: $x = 2$ and $x = 9$.</p>\n<p>(b) Check in the original equation:<br>• $x = 9$: $\\sqrt{9 + 7} = \\sqrt{16} = 4$ and $9 - 5 = 4$. ✓ Works.<br>• $x = 2$: $\\sqrt{2 + 7} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ but $2 - 5 = -3$. ✗ Fails.</p>\n<p>$x = 2$ is extraneous. Squaring erased the sign information: the squared equation also captures $\\sqrt{x+7} = -(x-5)$, and a square root (nonnegative) can never equal the negative number $x - 5 = -3$.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Final answer: The tray&#x27;s side length is $9$ inches; $x = 2$ is an extraneous solution introduced by squaring.</strong></p>",
 "S8-2": "<p>(a) Add component-wise (series impedances add):<br>$$Z_1 + Z_2 = (5 + 3i) + (2 - 7i) = (5 + 2) + (3 - 7)i = 7 - 4i\\ \\text{ohms}$$</p>\n<p>(b) The real part, $7$ ohms, is the total resistance of the two circuits in series. The imaginary part, $-4$ ohms, is the net reactance: the capacitive effect outweighs the inductive effect by $4$ ohms.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The total impedance is $7 - 4i$ ohms — $7$ ohms of resistance with a net reactance of $-4$ ohms.</strong></p>",
 "S8-3": "<p>(a) By the Remainder Theorem, the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 2$ is $P(2)$:<br>$$P(2) = 2(2)^3 - 3(2)^2 - 8(2) + 12 = 16 - 12 - 16 + 12 = 0$$</p>\n<p>(b) Yes. A remainder of $0$ means $x - 2$ divides $P(x)$ exactly, so $(x - 2)$ is a factor of $P(x)$.</p>\n<p>(c) $P$ is measured in hundreds of dollars, so $P(2) = 0$ means that at a ticket price of $\\$2$ the booth&#x27;s profit is $\\$0$ — the break-even price.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $P(2) = 0$; $(x - 2)$ is a factor of $P(x)$, and $\\$2$ is the booth&#x27;s break-even ticket price.</strong></p>",
 "S8-4": "<p>(a) Factor the difference of squares and cancel common factors:<br>$$\\frac{x^2 - 9}{x + 4} \\cdot \\frac{x + 4}{x - 3} = \\frac{(x + 3)(x - 3)}{x + 4} \\cdot \\frac{x + 4}{x - 3} = x + 3$$</p>\n<p>(b) The original denominators require $x + 4 \\neq 0$ and $x - 3 \\neq 0$, so $x \\neq -4$ and $x \\neq 3$. The value $x = -4$ must still be excluded even though the factor $(x + 4)$ canceled.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The product simplifies to $x + 3$, valid for $x \\neq -4, 3$.</strong></p>",
 "S8-5": "<p>(a) Treat the sum of squares as a difference of squares with $i^2 = -1$:<br>$$x^2 + 25 = x^2 - (5i)^2 = (x + 5i)(x - 5i)$$<br>(Check: $(x + 5i)(x - 5i) = x^2 - 25i^2 = x^2 + 25$. ✓)</p>\n<p>(b) Setting each factor to zero: $x = -5i$ or $x = 5i$. The solutions of $x^2 + 25 = 0$ are $x = \\pm 5i$.</p>\n<p>(c) For real $x$, $x^2 \\ge 0$, so $x^2 + 25 \\ge 25 &gt; 0$: the parabola $y = x^2 + 25$ has vertex $(0, 25)$ and opens upward, so it never reaches the $x$-axis. No real roots and no $x$-intercepts are the same fact stated algebraically and graphically; the roots exist but are nonreal.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $x^2 + 25 = (x + 5i)(x - 5i)$, with complex solutions $x = 5i$ and $x = -5i$; the graph has no $x$-intercepts because its minimum value is $25$.</strong></p>",
 "S8-6": "<p>(a) $P(120) = 2.5(120) - 180 = 300 - 180 = 120$. Interpretation: selling $120$ hot dogs produces a profit of $\\$120$.</p>\n<p>(b) Set $P(x) = 270$:<br>$$2.5x - 180 = 270 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 2.5x = 450 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 180$$</p>\n<p>(c) The $-180$ is the stand&#x27;s fixed cost of $\\$180$ (supplies, permit, equipment) that must be covered before any profit is made.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $P(120) = \\$120$ of profit; the stand must sell $180$ hot dogs to make a $\\$270$ profit.</strong></p>",
 "S8-7": "<p>(a) Row $n$ has the first row&#x27;s $12$ seats plus $2$ seats for each of the $n - 1$ steps up:<br>$$a(n) = 12 + 2(n - 1) = 2n + 10$$</p>\n<p>(b) $a(15) = 2(15) + 10 = 40$ seats.</p>\n<p>(c) $2n + 10 = 34 \\Rightarrow 2n = 24 \\Rightarrow n = 12$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $a(n) = 2n + 10$; row $15$ has $40$ seats, and the row with exactly $34$ seats is row $12$.</strong></p>",
 "S8-8": "<p>(a) Zeros: $x = -2$, $x = 1$, $x = 4$ (from the three factors). For the track, these are the horizontal positions where the height is $0$ — where the track meets ground level.</p>\n<p>(b) $y$-intercept: $f(0) = (2)(-1)(-4) = 8$.</p>\n<p>(c) $f$ is a cubic with positive leading coefficient, so as $x \\to -\\infty$, $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ (left end falls) and as $x \\to \\infty$, $f(x) \\to \\infty$ (right end rises).</p>\n<p>(d) Sketch: the curve comes up from the lower left, crosses the axis at $-2$, rises through the $y$-intercept $(0, 8)$, turns and dips to cross at $1$, reaches a local minimum below the axis, then rises to cross at $4$ and continues up to the right.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Zeros at $-2$, $1$, $4$ (ground-level crossings); $y$-intercept $8$ m; the track profile falls on the far left and rises on the far right.</strong></p>",
 "S8-9": "<p>(a) From the table, $f(2) = 24$: baking $2$ dozen cookies takes $24$ minutes.</p>\n<p>(b) $f^{-1}(30)$ asks for the input whose output is $30$. The table shows $f(3) = 30$, so $f^{-1}(30) = 3$: a baking time of $30$ minutes corresponds to $3$ dozen cookies.</p>\n<p>(c) $f^{-1}$ takes a time in minutes as input and gives the number of dozen cookies as output — exactly the reverse of $f$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f(2) = 24$ minutes; $f^{-1}(30) = 3$ dozen cookies.</strong></p>",
 "S8-10": "<p>(a) $800(1.35)^t = 10{,}000$.</p>\n<p>(b) Divide by $800$: $(1.35)^t = 12.5$. Express the solution as a logarithm and evaluate:<br>$$t = \\frac{\\log 12.5}{\\log 1.35} = \\frac{1.0969}{0.1303} \\approx 8.42$$</p>\n<p>(c) The culture reaches $10{,}000$ thousand cells (ten million cells) about $8.42$ hours — roughly $8$ hours $25$ minutes — after feeding.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $t = \\dfrac{\\log 12.5}{\\log 1.35} \\approx 8.42$ hours after feeding.</strong></p>",
 "S8-11": "<p>(a) Every point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the segment&#x27;s two endpoints. So a point on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ is equidistant from $A$ and $B$, and a point on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AC}$ is equidistant from $A$ and $C$. The two bisectors meet at a single point (the sides are not parallel, so neither are their bisectors), and that point is equidistant from all three vertices: $PA = PB = PC$. It is therefore the center of the unique circle through $A$, $B$, and $C$ — the circumcenter — and the third side&#x27;s bisector passes through it as well.</p>\n<p>(b) Perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$: midpoint $(3, 0)$, and $AB$ is horizontal, so the bisector is $x = 3$.<br>Perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AC}$: midpoint of $A(0,0)$ and $C(2,4)$ is $(1, 2)$; slope of $AC$ is $2$, so the bisector&#x27;s slope is $-\\tfrac{1}{2}$: $y - 2 = -\\tfrac{1}{2}(x - 1)$. At $x = 3$: $y = 2 - \\tfrac{1}{2}(2) = 1$.<br>Center: $(3, 1)$. Radius: distance to $A$: $\\sqrt{(3 - 0)^2 + (1 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16$ m.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Place the water station at $(3, 1)$; it is $\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16$ m from each stand, and it is the center of the circumscribed circle through the three stands.</strong></p>",
 "S8-12": "<p>(a) For $\\theta(t) = 36\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}t\\right)$:<br>• Amplitude $= 36$ — the ship swings at most $36^\\circ$ to either side of vertical.<br>• Midline $= 0$ — the vertical (upright, rest) position the ship passes through each swing.<br>• Period $= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi/5} = 10$ seconds — one complete back-and-forth swing takes $10$ seconds.</p>\n<p>(b) The greatest angle from vertical is the amplitude: $36^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(c) Solve $36\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}t\\right) = 18$: $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}t\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$, giving $t = \\dfrac{5}{6} \\approx 0.83$ seconds.</p>\n<p>(d) $\\dfrac{50}{10} = 5$ complete swings in $50$ seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Amplitude $36^\\circ$, midline $0^\\circ$ (vertical), period $10$ s; the ship first reaches $18^\\circ$ from vertical at $t = \\tfrac{5}{6} \\approx 0.83$ s, and makes $5$ complete swings in $50$ seconds.</strong></p>",
 "S8-13": "<p>(a) Let $(x, y)$ be a point on the parabola. Its distance to the focus $(0, 2)$ must equal its distance to the directrix $y = -2$:<br>$$\\sqrt{x^2 + (y - 2)^2} = |y + 2|$$<br>Square both sides: $x^2 + (y - 2)^2 = (y + 2)^2 \\Rightarrow x^2 + y^2 - 4y + 4 = y^2 + 4y + 4 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 8y$.</p>\n<p>(b) At the focus&#x27;s height, $y = 2$: $x^2 = 16$, so $x = \\pm 4$. The dish is $4 - (-4) = 8$ inches wide at that height.</p>\n<p>(c) A rim $18$ inches wide means $x = 9$ at the rim: $81 = 8y \\Rightarrow y = \\dfrac{81}{8} = 10.125$ inches.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The dish follows $x^2 = 8y$; it is $8$ inches wide at the focus and about $10.13$ inches deep at an $18$-inch rim.</strong></p>",
 "S8-14": "<p>(a) $50^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{18}$ radians. The radius is half the diameter: $r = 6$ in.</p>\n<p>(b) Sector area: $A = \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2}(36)\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{18}\\right) = 5\\pi \\approx 15.7\\ \\text{in}^2$.</p>\n<p>(c) Arc length (crust): $s = r\\theta = 6 \\times \\dfrac{5\\pi}{18} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} \\approx 5.2$ in.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The slice has area $5\\pi \\approx 15.7$ square inches and a crust of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} \\approx 5.2$ inches.</strong></p>",
 "S8-15": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> Parallelogram $ABCD$; diagonals $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ intersect at $E$.<br><strong>Prove:</strong> $AE = EC$ and $BE = ED$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statements</th><th>Reasons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$</td><td>1. Definition of a parallelogram</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle BAE \\cong \\angle DCE$</td><td>2. Alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines $AB$ and $CD$ cut by transversal $AC$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\angle ABE \\cong \\angle CDE$</td><td>3. Alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines $AB$ and $CD$ cut by transversal $BD$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $AB = CD$</td><td>4. Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $\\triangle AEB \\cong \\triangle CED$</td><td>5. ASA congruence (steps 2, 4, 3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. $AE = EC$ and $BE = ED$</td><td>6. CPCTC (corresponding parts of congruent triangles)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Conclusion: The diagonals $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BD}$ bisect each other at $E$. ∎</strong></p>",
 "S8-16": "<p>(a) Rectangle: $10 \\times 6 = 60\\ \\text{m}^2$. Semicircle: the diameter is $6$ m, so the radius is $3$ m, and its area is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\pi(3)^2 = 4.5\\pi \\approx 14.14\\ \\text{m}^2$.<br>Total area $= 60 + 4.5\\pi \\approx 74.1\\ \\text{m}^2$.</p>\n<p>(b) Bags needed: $74.1 \\div 8 \\approx 9.27$. Since partial bags can&#x27;t be bought, round <strong>up</strong> to $10$ bags.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The plot&#x27;s area is $60 + 4.5\\pi \\approx 74.1\\ \\text{m}^2$, and the garden club must buy $10$ bags of mulch.</strong></p>",
 "S8-17": "<p>(a) Study A uses <strong>random selection</strong> (students chosen at random from the enrollment list). Study B uses <strong>random assignment</strong> (volunteers assigned at random to a menu condition).</p>\n<p>(b) Study A&#x27;s results can be generalized to all district students, because its sample was randomly selected from that population, making it representative. Study B&#x27;s volunteers were not randomly selected from the district, so its results generalize only to students like the volunteers.</p>\n<p>(c) Study B can support a causal conclusion: random assignment balances the two groups on other factors ( tastes, grade level, hunger), so a satisfaction difference can be attributed to the menu itself. Study A imposes no treatment at all — it only measures opinions — so it cannot address cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Random selection in Study A supports generalizing to the district; random assignment in Study B supports concluding the new menu causes the satisfaction difference.</strong></p>",
 "S8-18": "<p>(a) $V = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi (3)^2 (12) = 36\\pi \\approx 113.1\\ \\text{cm}^3$.</p>\n<p>(b) Since $1\\ \\text{cm}^3 = 1\\ \\text{mL}$, one serving uses about $113.1$ mL of ice.</p>\n<p>(c) $10$ L $= 10{,}000$ mL. Servings: $10{,}000 \\div 113.1 \\approx 88.4$, so the vendor can make $88$ full servings.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The cup holds $36\\pi \\approx 113.1\\ \\text{cm}^3$ (about $113.1$ mL) of ice, and a $10$-liter block yields $88$ full servings.</strong></p>",
 "S8-19": "<p>(a) The observed difference is $7.8 - 7.3 = 0.5$ points in favor of the new recipe. The simulation models how large a difference in group means would arise from random assignment alone if the frosting made no difference at all — it repeatedly re-shuffles the actual scores into two groups of $20$ and records the difference.</p>\n<p>(b) Proportion of simulated differences at least as large as observed: $\\dfrac{105}{500} = 0.21 = 21\\%$. Since $21\\% &gt; 5\\%$, the difference is <strong>not</strong> statistically significant at the $5\\%$ level — a gap this big would occur by chance in about one random assignment out of five even if the frostings were equally liked.</p>\n<p>(c) The organizer should conclude that this experiment does <strong>not</strong> provide convincing evidence that judges prefer the new frosting; the observed $0.5$-point edge is well within the range of ordinary random-assignment variability.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Observed difference $0.5$ points; simulation p-value $\\approx 21\\% &gt; 5\\%$, so the difference is not statistically significant — no convincing evidence the new frosting is better liked.</strong></p>",
 "S8-20": "<p>(a) Meters of fencing: $\\$360 \\div \\$10/\\text{m} = 36$ m.</p>\n<p>(b) The side parallel to the wall gets the remaining fencing: $36 - 2x$ meters. Area:<br>$$A(x) = x(36 - 2x) = -2x^2 + 36x$$</p>\n<p>(c) The parabola opens downward; its vertex is at $x = \\dfrac{-36}{2(-2)} = 9$. Then the parallel side is $36 - 2(9) = 18$ m, and the maximum area is $A(9) = 9 \\times 18 = 162\\ \\text{m}^2$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The club can buy $36$ m of fencing; the area is maximized by two $9$-m sides perpendicular to the wall and an $18$-m side parallel to it, giving a maximum area of $162\\ \\text{m}^2$.</strong></p>",
 "S9-1": "<p>Factor by grouping:<br>$$t^3 - 4t^2 - 11t + 30 = (t - 2)(t^2 - 2t - 15) = (t - 2)(t - 5)(t + 3).$$<br>(Check: $(t-2)(t-5)(t+3)$ expands to $t^3 - 4t^2 - 11t + 30$. ✓)</p>\n<p>(a) Zeros: $t = 2$, $t = 5$, and $t = -3$.</p>\n<p>(b) Only $t = 2$ and $t = 5$ lie in the domain $t \\geq 0$: on days 2 and 5 after the valve opens, the net inflow is zero — inflow and outflow exactly balance. The zero at $t = -3$ describes a time before the valve opened and has no physical meaning here.</p>\n<p>(c) End behavior: the leading term $t^3$ dominates, so as $t \\to \\infty$, $P(t) \\to \\infty$ (and as $t \\to -\\infty$, $P(t) \\to -\\infty$). The rough graph for $t \\geq 0$ starts at the vertical intercept $P(0) = 30$ (30 thousand gallons per day), decreases through the $t$-axis at $t = 2$, dips below the axis, returns through the axis at $t = 5$, and then rises without bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $P(t) = (t-2)(t-5)(t+3)$ with zeros $2$, $5$, $-3$; in context, the reservoir&#x27;s net inflow is zero on days 2 and 5, and the model&#x27;s inflow ultimately grows without bound.</strong></p>",
 "S9-2": "<p>(a) Each member&#x27;s share is $\\dfrac{600}{x}$ dollars.</p>\n<p>(b) With 5 more members the share is $\\dfrac{600}{x+5}$, and the drop is $10:<br>$$\\frac{600}{x} - \\frac{600}{x+5} = 10.$$<br>Multiply through by $x(x+5)$:<br>$$600(x+5) - 600x = 10x(x+5) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 3000 = 10x^2 + 50x \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x^2 + 5x - 300 = 0.$$<br>Factor: $(x + 20)(x - 15) = 0$, so $x = 15$ or $x = -20$. The solution $x = -20$ is rejected: a number of members cannot be negative (it arises from the algebra, not from the situation).</p>\n<p>(c) Check: with 15 members each pays $600/15 = \\$40$; with 20 members each pays $600/20 = \\$30$, exactly $10 less. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the association originally has 15 members, each paying $40 toward the $600 compost system.</strong></p>",
 "S9-3": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of $3 - i$, which is $3 + i$:<br>$$Z = \\frac{8 + 6i}{3 - i} \\cdot \\frac{3 + i}{3 + i} = \\frac{(8 + 6i)(3 + i)}{3^2 + 1^2} = \\frac{24 + 8i + 18i + 6i^2}{10} = \\frac{24 + 26i - 6}{10} = \\frac{18 + 26i}{10} = 1.8 + 2.6i.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $Z = 1.8 + 2.6i$ ohms — the impedance has real part 1.8 ohms and imaginary part 2.6 ohms.</strong></p>",
 "S9-4": "<p>Rewrite the division as multiplication by the reciprocal, then factor everything:<br>$$\\frac{2x^2 - 8}{x^2 - x - 6} \\cdot \\frac{x - 3}{x + 2} = \\frac{2(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - 3)(x + 2)} \\cdot \\frac{x - 3}{x + 2}.$$<br>Here the first numerator is factored as $2(x^2 - 4) = 2(x-2)(x+2)$ and the first denominator as $(x-3)(x+2)$. Cancel the common factors $(x+2)$ and $(x-3)$:<br>$$= \\frac{2(x - 2)}{x + 2}.$$<br>Excluded values come from every denominator that appears: $x^2 - x - 6 \\neq 0$ gives $x \\neq 3, -2$; the divisor&#x27;s denominator gives $x \\neq 3$; and the divisor itself cannot be zero, so $x + 2 \\neq 0$ gives $x \\neq -2$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the quotient simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x-2)}{x+2}$, valid for $x \\neq -2$ and $x \\neq 3$.</strong></p>",
 "S9-5": "<p>(a) The constant $250$ is a fixed cost of $\\$250$ paid no matter how many trees are planted (permit and tool rental). The term $18x$ is the per-tree cost: $18$ dollars per tree for the sapling and planting labor, so it grows in direct proportion to the number of trees. The term $0.25x^2$ is an additional cost — such as irrigation and maintenance — that grows quadratically: each additional tree costs more to support than the last, because the term&#x27;s growth speeds up as $x$ increases (for example, it contributes $\\$25$ at 10 trees but $\\$400$ at 40 trees, sixteen times as much for four times the trees).</p>\n<p>(b) $C(40) = 0.25(40)^2 + 18(40) + 250 = 400 + 720 + 250 = 1370$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: planting 40 trees costs $\\$1{,}370$ in total — $\\$250$ fixed, $\\$720$ of per-tree cost, and $\\$400$ of the quadratically growing cost.</strong></p>",
 "S9-6": "<p>(a) Array A starts at $A(0) = 120$ kWh and increases by 15 kWh per month. Array B starts at 100 kWh and increases by $118 - 100 = 18$ kWh per month (the table rises by 18 each month).</p>\n<p>(b) Month 4: $A(4) = 120 + 15(4) = 180$ kWh; $B(4) = 172$ kWh. Array A produces more, by $180 - 172 = 8$ kWh.</p>\n<p>(c) Extend B&#x27;s pattern: $B(t) = 100 + 18t$. Solve $100 + 18t &gt; 120 + 15t$: $3t &gt; 20$, so $t &gt; 6.67$. The first whole month is $t = 7$: check $A(7) = 225$ kWh and $B(7) = 226$ kWh. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: A starts higher (120 vs. 100 kWh) but grows more slowly (15 vs. 18 kWh/month); A still leads by 8 kWh in month 4, and B first pulls ahead in month 7.</strong></p>",
 "S9-7": "<p>Compute $f(g(x))$: <br>$$f(g(x)) = \\frac{9}{5}\\left[\\frac{5}{9}(x - 32)\\right] + 32 = (x - 32) + 32 = x.$$<br>Compute $g(f(x))$:<br>$$g(f(x)) = \\frac{5}{9}\\left[\\left(\\frac{9}{5}x + 32\\right) - 32\\right] = \\frac{5}{9} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}x = x.$$</p>\n<p>Both compositions simplify to $x$: applying one conversion and then the other returns the original temperature, in either order. That is exactly the defining property of inverse functions.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f(g(x)) = x$ and $g(f(x)) = x$, so $g$ is confirmed as the inverse of $f$ — the volunteer&#x27;s Celsius conversion is correct.</strong></p>",
 "S9-8": "<p>(a) $D(0) = 2.0$ feet — the depth when monitoring begins. This is the vertical intercept of the depth function.</p>\n<p>(b) Increasing on $[0, 3]$ (2.0 ft rising to 5.2 ft); constant on $[3, 4]$ (holding at 5.2 ft); decreasing on $[4, 8]$ (falling to 2.6 ft).</p>\n<p>(c) Maximum depth is 5.2 feet, occurring from hour 3 to hour 4.</p>\n<p>(d) Average rate of change over $[0, 3]$: $\\dfrac{D(3) - D(0)}{3 - 0} = \\dfrac{5.2 - 2.0}{3} = \\dfrac{3.2}{3} \\approx 1.07$ feet per hour.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the pond starts at 2.0 ft, rises at an average of about 1.07 ft per hour for the first 3 hours, peaks at 5.2 ft during hours 3–4, then drains steadily back to 2.6 ft by hour 8.</strong></p>",
 "S9-9": "<p>(a) The total area is the sum of the two sections&#x27; areas, so add the functions:<br>$$T(t) = W(t) + S(t) = (120 + 30t) + (2t^2 + 50) = 2t^2 + 30t + 170 \\text{ square meters.}$$</p>\n<p>(b) $T(4) = 2(16) + 30(4) + 170 = 32 + 120 + 170 = 322$ square meters. (Check from the parts: $W(4) = 240$ and $S(4) = 82$; $240 + 82 = 322$. ✓)</p>\n<p>(c) $T(5) = 2(25) + 30(5) + 170 = 50 + 150 + 170 = 370$ square meters, which is less than 500.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the total planted area is $T(t) = 2t^2 + 30t + 170$ m²; it reaches 322 m² after 4 years, and at 370 m² after 5 years the greenway does not yet meet the 500 m² grant requirement.</strong></p>",
 "S9-10": "<p>(a)</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$t$ (weeks)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$E(t) = 3 \\cdot 2^t$ (m²)</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>24</td><td>48</td><td>96</td><td>192</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$P(t) = t^2 + 4t + 10$ (m²)</td><td>10</td><td>15</td><td>22</td><td>31</td><td>42</td><td>55</td><td>70</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(b) The algae first exceeds the native plant in week 4: $E(4) = 48 &gt; 42 = P(4)$, after trailing at week 3 ($24 &lt; 31$).</p>\n<p>(c) The algae&#x27;s coverage doubles every week — it grows by a constant factor of 2 — while the polynomial&#x27;s weekly gains grow only modestly (5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15 m²). A quantity multiplied by a fixed factor greater than 1 each period eventually outruns any polynomial growth, so the crossover in the table was inevitable; after week 4 the gap widens rapidly (48–42, then 96–55, then 192–70).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the algae first covers more of the pond in week 4 (48 m² vs. 42 m²), and its constant doubling guarantees it stays ahead from then on.</strong></p>",
 "S9-11": "<p>On the unit circle, the point at angle $\\theta$ is $(\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta)$.</p>\n<p>(a) $\\theta = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ is in Quadrant II (between $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\pi$). Its reference angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so<br>$$\\sin\\frac{5\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{6} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\qquad \\tan\\frac{5\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1/2}{-\\sqrt{3}/2} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$$<br>Signs: in Quadrant II the point&#x27;s $y$-coordinate is positive (sine positive) and its $x$-coordinate is negative (cosine negative), so the tangent — their ratio — is negative.</p>\n<p>(b) $\\theta = \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$ is in Quadrant IV, with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$:<br>$$\\sin\\frac{7\\pi}{4} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\qquad \\cos\\frac{7\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\qquad \\tan\\frac{7\\pi}{4} = -1.$$<br>Signs: in Quadrant IV the $x$-coordinate is positive and the $y$-coordinate is negative, so sine is negative, cosine positive, tangent negative.</p>\n<p>(c) $\\frac{13\\pi}{6} = 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{6}$: the blade has made one full turn plus $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so the marker is at exactly the same point of the unit circle as $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Therefore $\\sin\\frac{13\\pi}{6} = \\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$ — trigonometric values repeat every $2\\pi$ because the position on the circle repeats.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: at $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ (Quadrant II), $\\sin = \\frac12$, $\\cos = -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, $\\tan = -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{3}$; at $\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$ (Quadrant IV), $\\sin = -\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$, $\\cos = \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$, $\\tan = -1$; and $\\sin\\frac{13\\pi}{6} = \\frac12$ by one-full-turn periodicity.</strong></p>",
 "S9-12": "<p>(a) $\\angle APB$ is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $AB$, so the arc&#x27;s measure is twice the angle&#x27;s: $\\widehat{AB} = 2 \\times 38^\\circ = 76^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(b) The central angle $\\angle AOB$ intercepts the same arc, so it equals the arc measure: $\\angle AOB = 76^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(c) $\\angle AQB$ also is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $AB$, so $\\angle AQB = \\tfrac{1}{2}(76^\\circ) = 38^\\circ$. Any inscribed angle intercepting the same arc has the same measure.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: arc $AB$ measures $76^\\circ$, the central angle is $76^\\circ$, and the second seat&#x27;s viewing angle is also $38^\\circ$ — every seat on that arc sees the stage under the same angle.</strong></p>",
 "S9-13": "<p>(a) The radius is the distance from $(2, -3)$ to $(6, 0)$:<br>$$r = \\sqrt{(6 - 2)^2 + (0 - (-3))^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ miles.}$$<br>The circle&#x27;s equation is<br>$$(x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 25.$$</p>\n<p>(b) Distance from the center to $(-1, 1)$:<br>$$d = \\sqrt{(-1 - 2)^2 + (1 - (-3))^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ miles.}$$<br>Since $d = r$, the home lies exactly on the boundary. (Check in the equation: $(-1-2)^2 + (1+3)^2 = 9 + 16 = 25$. ✓)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the coverage boundary is $(x-2)^2 + (y+3)^2 = 25$, a circle of radius 5 miles, and the home at $(-1, 1)$ sits exactly on the boundary of the siren&#x27;s range.</strong></p>",
 "S9-14": "<p>(a) Midline: 5 feet — the average depth about which the tide oscillates. Amplitude: 2.5 feet — the depth rises and falls at most 2.5 feet from that average. Period: $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\pi/6} = 12$ hours — one complete tidal cycle takes 12 hours.</p>\n<p>(b) Maximum depth: $5 + 2.5 = 7.5$ feet, when the cosine equals 1 (at $t = 0$, midnight, and again at $t = 12$). Minimum depth: $5 - 2.5 = 2.5$ feet, when the cosine equals $-1$, first at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}t = \\pi$, i.e., $t = 6$ — 6:00 AM.</p>\n<p>(c) At $t = 3$: $D(3) = 5 + 2.5\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\cdot 3\\right) = 5 + 2.5\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 5 + 2.5(0) = 5$ feet.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the pier&#x27;s depth cycles every 12 hours between 2.5 ft (first at 6:00 AM) and 7.5 ft around a midline of 5 ft, and at 3:00 AM the depth is exactly the midline value, 5 ft.</strong></p>",
 "S9-15": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> $\\triangle ABC$ with $AB \\cong AC$; $AD$ is the bisector of $\\angle A$, with $D$ on $BC$.<br><strong>Prove:</strong> $\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statement</th><th>Reason</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $AB \\cong AC$</td><td>Given</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle BAD \\cong \\angle CAD$</td><td>$AD$ bisects $\\angle A$ (definition of angle bisector)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $AD \\cong AD$</td><td>Reflexive property</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $\\triangle ABD \\cong \\triangle ACD$</td><td>SAS congruence (steps 1, 2, 3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$</td><td>CPCTC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the base angles of the isosceles gable are congruent, $\\angle B \\cong \\angle C$, proved by showing the angle bisector splits the triangle into two SAS-congruent halves.</strong></p>",
 "S9-16": "<p>(a) Margin of error $\\approx 2 \\times 0.6 = 1.2$ kg.</p>\n<p>(b) Interval: $8.4 - 1.2 = 7.2$ kg to $8.4 + 1.2 = 9.6$ kg. (This matches the simulation&#x27;s middle-95% range of 7.2 to 9.6 kg.)</p>\n<p>(c) The entire interval lies above 7 kg — even the lowest plausible value for the true mean is 7.2 kg — so the claim is reasonable: the data support an average above 7 kg per week, though the true mean could be anywhere in the interval, not necessarily 8.4 kg.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the margin of error is about 1.2 kg, the true mean weekly recycling weight is plausibly between 7.2 kg and 9.6 kg per household, and the council&#x27;s &quot;more than 7 kg&quot; claim is reasonable because the whole interval exceeds 7 kg.</strong></p>",
 "S9-17": "<p>(a) Current density: $\\dfrac{18{,}400}{8.0} = 2{,}300$ people per square mile.</p>\n<p>(b) Development density: $\\dfrac{1{,}150}{0.25} = 4{,}600$ people per square mile.</p>\n<p>(c) New overall density: $\\dfrac{18{,}400 + 1{,}150}{8.0 + 0.25} = \\dfrac{19{,}550}{8.25} \\approx 2{,}370$ people per square mile. (Note: this is not the average of 2,300 and 4,600; a combined density must be total people over total area.)</p>\n<p>(d) $2{,}370 &lt; 2{,}500$, so the town would not yet qualify for frequent bus service under the guideline. The development itself, at 4,600 people/mi², is twice as dense as the town&#x27;s current 2,300 people/mi², but it is small enough that it raises the town-wide figure only modestly.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: current density 2,300 people/mi²; the development&#x27;s density is 4,600 people/mi²; the combined density would be about 2,370 people/mi² — still below the 2,500 people/mi² transit guideline.</strong></p>",
 "S9-18": "<p>(a) $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (3)^2 (8) = 72\\pi \\approx 226.19$ m³, so about $226$ m³.</p>\n<p>(b) $226.19 \\times 1{,}000 \\approx 226{,}195$ liters (using $72\\pi \\times 1000 \\approx 226{,}195$ L).</p>\n<p>(c) Daily demand: $500 \\times 40 = 20{,}000$ liters per day. Days supplied: $\\dfrac{226{,}195}{20{,}000} \\approx 11.3$ days, so the tank covers 11 full days of supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the tank holds $72\\pi \\approx 226$ m³, about 226,195 liters — enough for 11 full days at 20,000 liters per day for the 500 households.</strong></p>",
 "S9-19": "<p>(a) Sample proportion: $\\dfrac{78}{120} = 0.65$, so 65% of the sample supports the greenway.</p>\n<p>(b) Estimated supporters: $0.65 \\times 9{,}600 = 6{,}240$ residents. Because the 120 residents were chosen randomly, every resident had a comparable chance of selection, so the sample&#x27;s proportion is a reasonable stand-in for the town&#x27;s proportion — that is what licenses the generalization.</p>\n<p>(c) People already walking on a trail are self-selected trail users, who are far more likely to favor a greenway than a typical resident. The 92% figure describes trail users, not the town, so it would badly overstate town-wide support.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: 65% of the sample supports the project, giving an estimate of about 6,240 of the 9,600 residents; the random sample supports this inference, while the 92% trail poll does not because it sampled only existing trail users.</strong></p>",
 "S9-20": "<p>(a) A plane parallel to the base cuts the cone&#x27;s lateral surface in a closed curve at a constant scale of the base: the cross section is a <strong>circle</strong>, smaller than the base (a scaled copy of it).</p>\n<p>(b) A plane through the apex and perpendicular to the base passes through the cone&#x27;s axis and meets the lateral surface along two straight slant edges from the apex to the base: the cross section is an <strong>isosceles triangle</strong> whose base is a diameter of the cone&#x27;s base.</p>\n<p>(c) A slanted plane that cuts across all sides of the cone (missing both the apex and the base&#x27;s rim on one side) meets the curved surface in a smooth oval curve: the cross section is an <strong>ellipse</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the three cuts produce, in order, a circle, an isosceles triangle, and an ellipse — the shape depends entirely on how the cutting plane is oriented relative to the cone&#x27;s axis and base.</strong></p>",
 "S10-1": "<p>Substitute $u = x^2$ to reduce the quartic to a quadratic:<br>$$u^2 - 5u - 36 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (u - 9)(u + 4) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; u = 9 \\text{ or } u = -4.$$<br>Back-substitute: $x^2 = 9$ gives $x = 3$ or $x = -3$; $x^2 = -4$ gives $x = 2i$ or $x = -2i$. The complete factorization uses the sum-of-squares identity $x^2 + 4 = (x - 2i)(x + 2i)$:<br>$$P(x) = (x - 3)(x + 3)(x - 2i)(x + 2i).$$</p>\n<p>(b) $P$ has degree 4, so the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra guarantees exactly 4 complex solutions counting multiplicity — and we found exactly 4: $3, -3, 2i, -2i$.</p>\n<p>(c) Attendance cannot be negative or imaginary, so the meaningful solution is $x = 3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the solutions are $x = 3$, $x = -3$, $x = 2i$, and $x = -2i$ — four solutions, as the degree requires; in context, the festival breaks even at an attendance of 3 thousand people.</strong></p>",
 "S10-2": "<p>Long division of $2x^2 + 3x - 5$ by $x + 2$:</p>\n<ul><li>$2x^2 \\div x = 2x$. Multiply: $2x(x + 2) = 2x^2 + 4x$. Subtract: $(2x^2 + 3x) - (2x^2 + 4x) = -x$. Bring down $-5$.</li><li>$-x \\div x = -1$. Multiply: $-1(x + 2) = -x - 2$. Subtract: $(-x - 5) - (-x - 2) = -3$.</li></ul>\n<p>So the quotient is $2x - 1$ with remainder $-3$:<br>$$\\frac{2x^2 + 3x - 5}{x + 2} = 2x - 1 - \\frac{3}{x + 2}.$$<br>Verification: $(x + 2)(2x - 1) - 3 = 2x^2 + 4x - x - 2 - 3 = 2x^2 + 3x - 5$. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $\\dfrac{2x^2 + 3x - 5}{x + 2} = 2x - 1 - \\dfrac{3}{x + 2}$, confirmed by multiplying back to the original expression.</strong></p>",
 "S10-3": "<p>(a) Wristband: $C = 28$. Pay-per-ride: $C = 3.5r$. The graphs are a horizontal line at height 28 and a line through the origin with slope 3.5.</p>\n<p>(b) Set the costs equal: $3.5r = 28 \\Rightarrow r = 8$, with $C = 28$. The graphs intersect at $(8, 28)$.</p>\n<p>(c) At the intersection point, the same $r$-value makes both cost expressions equal to the same $C$ — that is, the point lies on both graphs exactly when its $r$-coordinate satisfies $28 = 3.5r$. So the intersection&#x27;s $r$-coordinate is that equation&#x27;s solution. For 5 rides: pay-per-ride costs $3.5(5) = \\$17.50 &lt; \\$28$, so pay-per-ride is cheaper. For 12 rides: pay-per-ride costs $3.5(12) = \\$42 &gt; \\$28$, so the wristband is cheaper.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the plans break even at 8 rides ($28 either way); below 8 rides pay-per-ride wins, and above 8 rides the wristband wins.</strong></p>",
 "S10-4": "<p>Factor the first denominator: $x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2)$. Rewrite the second fraction with that common denominator:<br>$$\\frac{x}{(x-2)(x+2)} + \\frac{2(x - 2)}{(x+2)(x-2)} = \\frac{x + 2x - 4}{(x-2)(x+2)} = \\frac{3x - 4}{(x-2)(x+2)}.$$<br>The numerator $3x - 4$ shares no factor with the denominator, so the result is in lowest terms. Excluded values: $x \\neq 2$ and $x \\neq -2$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the combined rate simplifies to $\\dfrac{3x - 4}{(x-2)(x+2)}$, valid for $x \\neq -2$ and $x \\neq 2$.</strong></p>",
 "S10-5": "<p>(a) Starting from $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$, multiply both sides by 3: $3V = \\pi r^2 h$. Divide both sides by $\\pi r^2$:<br>$$h = \\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}.$$</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute $V = 36\\pi$ and $r = 3$:<br>$$h = \\frac{3(36\\pi)}{\\pi (3)^2} = \\frac{108\\pi}{9\\pi} = 12.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: solving for the height gives $h = \\dfrac{3V}{\\pi r^2}$, and the sand sculpture stands 12 feet tall.</strong></p>",
 "S10-6": "<p>(a) In $y = -2(x - 3)^2 + 5$ relative to $p(x) = x^2$: the negative sign reflects the graph across the $x$-axis (the arch opens downward); the factor 2 stretches it vertically by a factor of 2; $(x - 3)$ shifts it 3 units right; $+5$ shifts it 5 units up. The vertex is $(3, 5)$ — the top of the arch, 5 units high at its center.</p>\n<p>(b) For $h(x) = x^3 - 4x$: $h(-x) = (-x)^3 - 4(-x) = -x^3 + 4x = -(x^3 - 4x) = -h(x)$. Since $h(-x) = -h(x)$, $h$ is <strong>odd</strong>, and its graph is symmetric about the origin.<br>For $k(x) = x^2 + 6$: $k(-x) = (-x)^2 + 6 = x^2 + 6 = k(x)$. Since $k(-x) = k(x)$, $k$ is <strong>even</strong>, and its graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the arch is the parent parabola reflected, stretched by 2, shifted right 3 and up 5, with vertex $(3, 5)$; $h$ is odd (origin symmetry) and $k$ is even ($y$-axis symmetry).</strong></p>",
 "S10-7": "<p>Set $V(t) = 10{,}000$:<br>$$200(1.8)^t = 10{,}000 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (1.8)^t = 50.$$</p>\n<p>(a) Exact time as a logarithm: $t = \\log_{1.8}(50) = \\dfrac{\\log 50}{\\log 1.8}$.</p>\n<p>(b) Evaluate: $t = \\dfrac{1.69897}{0.25527} \\approx 6.66$, so about $6.7$ hours. Check: $V(6.66) = 200(1.8)^{6.66} \\approx 200(50.0) \\approx 10{,}000$ viewers. ✓</p>\n<p>(c) The livestream, starting from 200 viewers and growing by a factor of 1.8 each hour, reaches 10,000 viewers after roughly six and two-thirds hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $t = \\dfrac{\\log 50}{\\log 1.8} \\approx 6.7$ hours for the audience to grow from 200 to 10,000 viewers.</strong></p>",
 "S10-8": "<p>(a) <br>$$P(t) = \\begin{cases} 5, &amp; 0 &lt; t \\leq 1 \\\\ 8, &amp; 1 &lt; t \\leq 2 \\\\ 11, &amp; 2 &lt; t \\leq 3 \\\\ 14, &amp; 3 &lt; t \\leq 4 \\end{cases}$$</p>\n<p>(b) The graph is four horizontal steps at heights 5, 8, 11, 14. Each step has a closed circle at its right endpoint (e.g., $(1, 5)$) and an open circle at its left endpoint (e.g., $(1, 8)$), because the higher price takes over the instant $t$ passes a whole hour.</p>\n<p>(c) $P(0.5) = \\$5$ (first hour). $P(2.5) = \\$11$ (third hour bracket). $P(3.2) = \\$14$ (fourth hour bracket). The jumps occur because the garage charges for any part of an hour as a full hour, so the cost leaps by $3 the moment a new hour begins rather than growing gradually.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: parking costs $5, $8, $11, or $14 depending on which hour-bracket the stay falls into — $5 for half an hour, $11 for 2.5 hours, $14 for 3.2 hours — with jumps at each whole hour.</strong></p>",
 "S10-9": "<p>(a) $f(3) = 55$: after 3 minutes, the volunteers have inflated 55 balloons in total.</p>\n<p>(b) $f^{-1}(30) = 2$: reading the table backward, the total reached 30 balloons at $x = 2$ minutes.</p>\n<p>(c) In inverse notation, the time to have 90 balloons ready is $f^{-1}(90) = 4$ minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $f(3) = 55$ balloons at 3 minutes; $f^{-1}(30) = 2$ means 30 balloons were ready at 2 minutes; and $f^{-1}(90) = 4$ means the 90-balloon target is met at 4 minutes.</strong></p>",
 "S10-10": "<p>(a) Domain: $[0, 40]$ seconds — the show segment runs from launch to landing over 40 seconds. Range: $[0, 120]$ feet — the height varies from ground level up to 120 feet.</p>\n<p>(b) $f(10) = 120$: at 10 seconds into the show, the drone reaches its maximum height of 120 feet.</p>\n<p>(c) The height is constant on $[10, 25]$ seconds: after climbing for 10 seconds, the drone hovers at 120 feet for 15 seconds before descending.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the height function has domain $[0, 40]$ s and range $[0, 120]$ ft; $f(10) = 120$ ft is the peak, held during a hover from $t = 10$ s to $t = 25$ s.</strong></p>",
 "S10-11": "<p>(a) Midline: 12 hours — the average daylight over the year. Amplitude: 2.8 hours — daylight varies up to 2.8 hours above and below that average. Period: 365 days — the daylight cycle repeats annually.</p>\n<p>(b) Maximum daylight: $12 + 2.8 = 14.8$ hours, when the sine equals 1: $\\frac{2\\pi}{365}(t - 80) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\Rightarrow t - 80 = \\frac{365}{4} = 91.25 \\Rightarrow t \\approx 171$. So the longest day is about day 171 (mid-to-late June), with 14.8 hours of daylight.</p>\n<p>(c) $D(80) = 12 + 2.8\\sin(0) = 12$ hours. Day 80 is the model&#x27;s phase-shift reference point: the sine is zero and daylight equals the annual average of 12 hours, crossing from below-average to above-average (the model&#x27;s spring equinox).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: daylight averages 12 hours, swings ±2.8 hours over a 365-day cycle, peaks at 14.8 hours around day 171, and equals exactly 12 hours on day 80.</strong></p>",
 "S10-12": "<p>(a) Derivation: For a fixed central angle, sectors of different circles are similar, and a sector with angle $\\theta$ radians is the fraction $\\dfrac{\\theta}{2\\pi}$ of its full circle (since the full circle is $2\\pi$ radians). The full circle&#x27;s area is $\\pi r^2$, so the sector&#x27;s area is<br>$$A = \\frac{\\theta}{2\\pi} \\cdot \\pi r^2 = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta.$$</p>\n<p>(b) With $r = 10$ m and $\\theta = 1.2$: $A = \\frac{1}{2}(10)^2(1.2) = 60$ square meters. The outer arc length is $s = r\\theta = 10 \\times 1.2 = 12$ meters.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the sector formula $A = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$ comes from taking the fraction $\\theta/(2\\pi)$ of the circle; the stage wedge has area 60 m² and an outer arc of 12 m.</strong></p>",
 "S10-13": "<p>(a) A point $(x, y)$ on the parabola is equidistant from the focus $(0, 3)$ and the directrix $y = -3$:<br>$$\\sqrt{x^2 + (y - 3)^2} = |y + 3|.$$<br>Square both sides and expand:<br>$$x^2 + y^2 - 6y + 9 = y^2 + 6y + 9 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x^2 = 12y.$$</p>\n<p>(b) At $x = 6$: $36 = 12y$, so $y = 3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the dish&#x27;s profile is $x^2 = 12y$, and at 6 feet from the center axis the rim sits 3 feet above the vertex.</strong></p>",
 "S10-14": "<p>(a) $\\angle AEB$ is formed by two chords intersecting inside the circle, so its measure is half the sum of the measures of the arcs intercepted by the angle and its vertical angle — arcs $AB$ and $CD$:<br>$$\\angle AEB = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(80^\\circ + 100^\\circ\\right) = 90^\\circ.$$</p>\n<p>(b) $\\angle APB$ is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $AB$:<br>$$\\angle APB = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\left(80^\\circ\\right) = 40^\\circ.$$</p>\n<p>(c) The answers differ because the angles&#x27; vertices sit in different places: $\\angle AEB$ has its vertex inside the circle, so it averages the two arcs the chord pair cuts off, while $\\angle APB$ has its vertex on the circle, so it takes half of just its one intercepted arc.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the crossing chords form a $90^\\circ$ angle, while the inscribed angle viewing arc $AB$ measures $40^\\circ$.</strong></p>",
 "S10-15": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> Rectangle $ABCD$ with diagonals $AC$ and $BD$.<br><strong>Prove:</strong> $AC \\cong BD$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statement</th><th>Reason</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $AD \\cong BC$</td><td>Opposite sides of a parallelogram (a rectangle is a parallelogram) are congruent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle DAB \\cong \\angle CBA$</td><td>All angles of a rectangle are right angles, so both measure $90^\\circ$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $AB \\cong BA$</td><td>Reflexive property</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $\\triangle DAB \\cong \\triangle CBA$</td><td>SAS congruence (steps 1, 2, 3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $DB \\cong CA$</td><td>CPCTC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Since $DB$ and $CA$ are exactly the diagonals $BD$ and $AC$, the diagonals are congruent. ∎</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $AC \\cong BD$ — the diagonals of a rectangle are always equal, which is why the builder&#x27;s diagonal check correctly detects a frame that is out of square.</strong></p>",
 "S10-16": "<p>(a) The fencing covers two perpendicular sides of length $x$ and one side parallel to the wall, so the parallel side has length $120 - 2x$ meters. The area is<br>$$A(x) = x(120 - 2x) = -2x^2 + 120x \\text{ square meters.}$$</p>\n<p>(b) $A$ is a downward-opening parabola, so its maximum is at the vertex: $x = \\dfrac{-120}{2(-2)} = 30$. Because the parabola opens downward, this critical point gives the largest possible area (areas at $x = 20$ and $x = 40$ are both $1{,}600$ m², less than the vertex value, confirming the peak).</p>\n<p>(c) With $x = 30$: the parallel side is $120 - 2(30) = 60$ meters, and $A(30) = 30 \\times 60 = 1{,}800$ square meters.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the garden should measure 30 m (perpendicular sides) by 60 m (parallel to the wall), giving a maximum area of 1,800 m² from the 120 m of fencing.</strong></p>",
 "S10-17": "<p>(a) This is an observational study based on a sample survey — no treatment was assigned; researchers simply asked people about their behavior. Worse, the data were collected by surveying only people at the farmers market.</p>\n<p>(b) Two reasons the causal claim fails: First, the sampling frame is biased — only market shoppers were surveyed, so the &quot;people who don&#x27;t shop there&quot; side of the comparison was never actually measured from a comparable sample, and shoppers are not representative of the town. Second, confounding (including self-selection and reverse causation): people who already care about healthy eating, cook at home more, or have higher incomes are more likely both to shop at a farmers market and to eat more vegetables — and people who already eat many vegetables may seek out the market because of that habit. The market may be a symptom of healthy habits rather than their cause.</p>\n<p>(c) A causal claim would need random assignment — for example, randomly selecting households to receive farmers-market vouchers (or a new market stall in their neighborhood) and comparing their eating habits with a randomly assigned control group that did not. Random assignment balances the confounders across groups, so a resulting difference could be attributed to the market.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the report rests on a biased, shopper-only observational survey with obvious confounders; it shows association at most, and only a randomized design could support the claim that the market causes healthier eating.</strong></p>",
 "S10-18": "<p>(a) Cylinder: $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi (6)^2 (4) = 144\\pi$ cubic feet. Hemisphere: $V = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi (6)^3 = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi (216) = 144\\pi$ cubic feet. Total exact volume: $144\\pi + 144\\pi = 288\\pi$ cubic feet.</p>\n<p>(b) $288\\pi \\approx 904.78$, so about $905$ cubic feet.</p>\n<p>(c) Capacity: $\\dfrac{904.78}{15} \\approx 60.3$, so the pavilion can hold 60 people under the standard (rounding down, since a 61st person would not get the required 15 ft³).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the pavilion encloses $288\\pi \\approx 905$ cubic feet of space — enough for 60 people at 15 cubic feet per person.</strong></p>",
 "S10-19": "<p>(a) Observed difference: $0.65 - 0.45 = 0.20$ — the door-hanger group participated at a rate 20 percentage points higher.</p>\n<p>(b) Proportion of simulations at least this large: $\\dfrac{18}{500} = 0.036$, or 3.6%.</p>\n<p>(c) Since $3.6\\% &lt; 5\\%$, the result is statistically significant at the 5% level. The simulation proportion means: if the invitation type truly made no difference, random assignment alone would produce a participation gap of 0.20 or larger only about 3.6% of the time. That is too rare to blame on chance, so the experiment gives convincing evidence that door hangers genuinely outperform emails for this event.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the observed advantage is 0.20 (65% vs. 45%); a gap that large arose in only 3.6% of no-difference simulations, below the 5% threshold — convincing evidence that door-hanger invitations work better.</strong></p>",
 "S10-20": "<p>(a) The cone&#x27;s slant height comes from the Pythagorean theorem with legs 3 cm (radius) and 4 cm (height):<br>$$l = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ cm.}$$</p>\n<p>(b) Surfaces counted: the cylinder&#x27;s lateral surface only (the bottom is open and the top is covered by the cone) and the cone&#x27;s lateral surface only (its base is the join with the cylinder, not exposed paper).<br>• Cylinder lateral area: $2\\pi r h = 2\\pi (3)(10) = 60\\pi$ cm².<br>• Cone lateral area: $\\pi r l = \\pi (3)(5) = 15\\pi$ cm².<br>• Total: $60\\pi + 15\\pi = 75\\pi \\approx 235.6$ cm².</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the lantern needs $75\\pi \\approx 235.6$ cm² of paper — $60\\pi$ cm² for the cylindrical side and $15\\pi$ cm² for the conical top with slant height 5 cm.</strong></p>"
};
